The error occurs because `toSignal` converts an Observable into a Signal, but does not automatically make it writable. To convert an Observable to a WritableSignal, you need to use the `signal` function directly on the value emitted by the Observable. Here's how you can modify your code:

```ts
import { Component, effect, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { AppService } from "../app.service";
import { toSignal } from "@angular/core/rxjs-interop";

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  templateUrl: './parent.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./parent.component.css']
})
export class ParentComponent {

  translations = signal({ data: [] });

  constructor(private appService: AppService) {
    this.translations = toSignal(this.appService.getTranslations()).asReadonly();
    effect(() => {
      console.log('translation API:', this.translations());
    });
  }

  changeValue(): void {
    this.translations.set({ data: { hello: 'hallo' } });
  }
}
```

In the constructor, `toSignal(this.appService.getTranslations()).asReadonly()` converts the Observable to a Signal and makes it read-only. To make it writable, use `signal` directly on the value emitted by the Observable:

```ts
this.translations = signal(toSignal(this.appService.getTranslations()).get());
```

Then you can update its value using `.set()` as shown in your `changeValue` method.

Remember to import and use `signal` from `@angular/core`.

