In your scenario, you can indeed implement the side effect directly using an Angular signal without needing to convert it into an observable. The `effect` utility from signals allows you to run a function whenever the signal changes, which is perfect for your use case.

Here’s how you can do it:

```typescript
import { effect } from '@angular/core';

// Assuming #isItEditor$ is now a signal
effect(() => {
  const funded = this.formGroup.controls.funded;
  if (this.#isItEditor$()) {
    funded.enable();
  } else {
    funded.disable();
  }
});
```

This `effect` will run whenever the value of `#isItEditor$` changes, enabling or disabling the `funded` control accordingly. This approach is more concise and leverages signals effectively for side effects, avoiding the need to manually manage subscriptions as you did with observables.

