The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular handles change detection with signals in `OnPush` mode. Even though you've disabled zone.js and updated the signal's value, Angular might not automatically trigger a view update because the component isn't marked for check.

To ensure the view updates, you need to manually mark the component for checking after updating the signal:

```typescript
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    {{ title() }} 
    Change 
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually mark the component for check
    this.ngZone.markForCheck();
  }
}
```

Additionally, ensure you import `NgZone` and inject it into your component:

```typescript
import { Component, NgZone, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    {{ title() }} 
    Change 
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');
  constructor(private ngZone: NgZone) {}

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually mark the component for check
    this.ngZone.markForCheck();
  }
}
```

This approach ensures Angular recognizes the change and updates the view accordingly.

