In Angular 16 with standalone components, routing needs to be configured differently compared to NgModule-based applications. The issue likely stems from how you're setting up your routes and integrating them into the application.

Firstly, ensure `app.routes.ts` is correctly set up:

```typescript
// app.routes.ts
import { Routes, RouterModule } from '@angular/router';
import { HomeComponent } from './home/home.component';

export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: '', component: HomeComponent }
];

export const routing = RouterModule.forRoot(routes);
```

Next, import and use `routing` in your `app.component.ts`:

```typescript
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { routing } from './app.routes';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: '<router-outlet></router-outlet>'
})
export class AppComponent {
  constructor() {
    // Ensure routing is initialized here if needed.
  }
}
```

Finally, make sure to include the `RouterModule` in your application's main module or standalone bootstrapping file:

```typescript
// app.main.ts (or equivalent)
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { routing } from './app.routes';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [routing]
});
```

This setup ensures that the router is properly initialized and routes are correctly applied. If you're still facing issues, double-check your imports and ensure there are no typos or missing dependencies.

