The issue lies in how you're updating the `houses` array signal. When you use `push`, it modifies the existing array reference, which doesn't trigger a change detection because Angular sees no new object reference. Instead, update the array by creating a new one with the changes:

```typescript
case "INSERT":
  this.houses.update(houses => {
    const updatedHouses = [...houses, payload.new];
    return updatedHouses;
  });
  break;
```

This creates a new array reference each time, ensuring Angular detects the change and triggers your effect. Similarly, for deletion:

```typescript
case "DELETE":
  this.houses.update(houses => houses.filter(house => house.id !== payload.old.id));
  break;
```

Both operations now correctly trigger the effect because they result in a new array reference.

Remember, effects run asynchronously during change detection, so ensure your signal updates are properly tracked.

