In Laravel, you can specify the controller's namespace either by using the `->namespace()` method in your route definition or by explicitly importing the fully qualified class name at the top of your routes file. Both methods are correct and serve the same purpose.

Given your scenario, specifying the namespace directly with `->namespace('App\Http\Controllers\Admin')` is a cleaner approach because it keeps your route definitions more readable without requiring additional imports. However, if you prefer to use the import statement for clarity or maintainability, that's also acceptable.

Here’s how you can define your routes using the namespace:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PhotoController;

Route::prefix('admin')->namespace('App\Http\Controllers\Admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

Alternatively, if you prefer to use import statements for clarity:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PhotoController;

Route::prefix('admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

Both methods ensure that Laravel correctly resolves the `PhotoController` class. Choose the method that best fits your coding style and project structure.

