To unit test an external mocked class with constructor parameters in Laravel without creating a factory, you can use the `resolve` method provided by Laravel's service container to pass constructor arguments directly. This approach simplifies your test code and reduces unnecessary complexity. Here’s how you can do it:

```php tab=Pest
use ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha;
use Mockery\MockInterface;

test('verify recaptcha', function () {
    $secret = config('services.recaptcha.secret');

    $mock = Mockery::mock(ReCaptcha::class, function (MockInterface $mock) use ($secret) {
        $mock->shouldReceive('make')->once()->andReturn($mock);
        $mock->shouldReceive('verify')->once()->andReturn(true); // Adjust the return value as needed
    });

    $this->instance(ReCaptcha::class, $mock);

    resolve(ReCaptcha::class, ['secret' => $secret])->verify();
});
```

This code snippet uses `Mockery` to create a mock of `ReCaptcha`, sets up expectations for its methods, and then resolves the class with the provided secret parameter. This approach avoids the need for a factory and keeps your test concise and focused on the functionality being tested.

