Your approach of using `ChangeDetectorRef` to manually trigger change detection for `OnPush` components is correct in this scenario. The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular handles change detection with `OnPush` strategy, especially when dealing with asynchronous operations like HTTP calls or events outside the component's scope.

In your `LoginComponent`, marking the detector for check (`this.ref.markForCheck()`) after updating `isSubmitting` is necessary because `OnPush` components only detect changes if their inputs change or if they are explicitly marked. Without calling `markForCheck`, Angular won't re-render the template even though `isSubmitting` has changed.

Here’s a concise version of your code snippet:

```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectorRef } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-login',
  templateUrl: './login.component.html',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
})
export class LoginComponent {
  isSubmitting = false;

  constructor(private ref: ChangeDetectorRef, private http: HttpClient) {}

  login() {
    this.isSubmitting = true;
    this.http.post('/api/login', { username: this.username.value, password: this.password.value })
      .subscribe({
        next: () => {
          this.isSubmitting = false; 
          this.ref.markForCheck(); // Explicitly mark for check
        },
        error: (err) => {
          console.error('Login failed:', err);
          this.isSubmitting = false;
          this.ref.markForCheck();
        }
      });
  }
}
```

Ensure your template correctly binds to `isSubmitting`:

```html
<button [disabled]="isSubmitting" mat-raised-button>
  {{'login' | translate}}
</button>
```

This setup ensures that the button's state updates when `isSubmitting` changes, even with `OnPush` change detection strategy.

