To set up locale-based routing in your Laravel Inertia application, you can use URL defaults as described in excerpt [6]. This approach allows you to define a default value for the `locale` parameter that will be applied during the current request without needing to pass it every time. Here’s how you can implement this:

1. **Define Middleware**: Create or modify your middleware to set the locale based on the URL.

```php
// File: app/Http/Middleware/SetDefaultLocale.php
namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL;

class SetDefaultLocale
{
    public function handle($request, Closure $next)
    {
        // Define default locale as 'en' and override with URL parameter if present.
        URL::defaults(['locale' => 'en', 'locale' => $request->route('locale')]);

        return $next($request);
    }
}
```

2. **Register Middleware**: Register the middleware in your `app/Http/Kernel.php`.

```php
protected $middlewareAliases = [
    // ...
    'setDefaultLocale' => \App\Http\Middleware\SetDefaultLocale::class,
];
```

3. **Update Routes**: Apply this middleware to your routes.

```php
// File: routes/web.php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;

Route::middleware(['setDefaultLocale'])->prefix('{locale?}')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('posts', PostController::class);
    Route::resource('comments', CommentController::class);
});
```

This setup ensures that the locale is correctly set based on the URL, with 'en' as the default. The `URL::defaults` method automatically handles the fallback to 'en' when no locale is provided in the URL.

