The issue arises from how Angular tracks changes in the `@for` loop using the `$index` as the tracking expression. When you click on a button, it triggers re-creation of the entire list, leading to a performance penalty and loss of state (like focus or selected text). This is because the identity-based tracking with `$index` causes Angular to treat each item as distinct, even if they have the same properties.

To fix this, use a unique identifier for tracking. Modify your `app.component.ts` to track by `todo.id`:

```typescript
@for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
  {{ todo.done }} -->
}
```

This ensures that Angular correctly identifies and updates only the specific item when its state changes.

Additionally, ensure your `SliderComponent` is properly configured for two-way binding. The component should emit an event with the updated value:

```typescript
// slider.component.ts
@Output() activeChange = new EventEmitter<boolean>();

onClick(event: Event) {
  this.active = !this.active;
  this.activeChange.emit(this.active);
}
```

In `app.component.ts`, bind to the output property correctly:

```html
<app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle($event, todo)"></app-slider>
```

And update your logic in `App` component accordingly:

```typescript
toggle(active: boolean, todo: Todo) {
  todo.done = active;
  this.buildTodos();
}
```

This approach ensures that only the intended item's state is updated.

