To achieve lazy loading for each iframe in your Angular application, you can use the `@defer` directive with appropriate triggers. Since you want iframes to load as the page scrolls or one by one, you should use the `on` trigger with a scroll event or set up individual `@defer` blocks.

Here’s how you can modify your code:

```angular-html
@for (link of youtubeLinks; track $index) {
  @defer (on viewport) {
    <iframe [src]="link" frameborder="0"></iframe>
  }
}
```

This will defer the loading of each iframe until it enters the viewport, improving initial load performance.

If you want iframes to load one by one as they are scrolled into view, consider using a custom trigger based on scroll events. However, Angular's built-in `@defer` does not directly support this out-of-the-box. You might need to implement a custom solution or use third-party libraries that handle dynamic loading more granularly.

For now, the above approach with `on viewport` should help reduce initial load time significantly.

