The behavior you're observing stems from how Angular handles references and copies in different contexts. When using `@for` with template input variables, Angular creates a new instance of the child component for each iteration, which can lead to unexpected reference issues if not handled correctly. Specifically, when you assign `activeProblem` to `problemGroup` inside the `ngOnInit` method within the `@for` block, it might be creating a shallow copy due to how template variables are managed.

In contrast, using `*ngFor` directly in your component's template ensures that the same instance of the child component is reused when possible. This means any mutations to `problemGroup` will reflect back to `activeProblem`, as they refer to the same object.

To ensure consistent behavior with `@for`, you can use a service or another mechanism to manage shared state, ensuring that changes in one component are reflected across all instances. Alternatively, consider using a single instance of the child component and managing its state through services or parent-to-child communication patterns like events or observables.

Here’s an example of how you might refactor your code to ensure consistent behavior:

```typescript
// Parent Component
<app-child *ngFor="let problem of problems; trackBy: trackById" [activeProblem]="problem"></app-child>

// Child Component
trackById(index: number, item: ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem): string {
  return item.id.toString();
}

ngOnInit(): void {
  if (this.activeProblem instanceof ProblemGroup) {
    this.problemGroup = this.activeProblem as ProblemGroup;
  } else {
    this.narrativeProblem = this.activeProblem;
  }
}
```

This approach ensures that the same instance of `activeProblem` is used across all child components, maintaining consistent state.

