In Angular 17's `@if` syntax, you can store the result of an expression in a variable using the `as` keyword within the same block. This allows you to use the stored value later in the template. Here’s how you can do it:

```angular-ts
@Component({
  ...
  template: `
    @if (checkServerStatus() as status) {
      {{ status }}
      <p>Based on the server status, we will display additional information.</p>
    }
  `,
})
class AppComponent {
  checkServerStatus() {
    // Your logic to determine the server status
    return 'running';
  }
}
```

In this example, `checkServerStatus()` is called within the `@if` block. The result of this function call is stored in the variable `status`. You can then use `status` directly in your template without needing an additional `@else` or `@default` block.

This approach leverages the `as` keyword to capture and reuse the value, making your template more readable and maintainable.

