In your scenario, the issue arises because signals in Angular are designed to work with change detection mechanisms, and disabling zone.js does not trigger change detection automatically. When you call `title.update()`, the value of the signal changes, but Angular's change detection might not run immediately without proper triggers.

To ensure the view updates when using signals outside of the Angular zone, you need to manually trigger change detection. You can do this by running the change detection explicitly after updating the signal:

```typescript
import { ChangeDetectorRef } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    {{ title() }} 
    Change 
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');
  constructor(private cdr: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually trigger change detection
    this.cdr.detectChanges();
  }
}
```

By injecting `ChangeDetectorRef` and calling `detectChanges()` after updating the signal, you ensure that Angular's change detector runs and updates the view accordingly.

