To retrieve the value of a route parameter in your controller method, you don't need to use `Request $request`. The parameter will be passed directly as an argument to your controller method if you're not using model binding. Here's how you can do it:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\UserController;

// Route definition...
Route::get('/user/{id}', [UserController::class, 'show']);

// Controller method definition...
public function show(string $id): View
{
    return view('user.profile', [
        'user' => User::findOrFail($id)
    ]);
}
```

In this example, the `{id}` parameter from your route is automatically passed as an argument to the `show` method in the `UserController`. This works because you've defined it after any other dependencies (like `Request $request`) and before the method signature. If you need to use a `Request` instance for other purposes within the method, you can still type-hint it:

```php
public function show(Request $request, string $id): View
{
    // You can access route parameters directly as arguments.
    return view('user.profile', [
        'user' => User::findOrFail($id)
    ]);
}
```

This approach simplifies your method signature and leverages Laravel's automatic dependency injection for route parameters.

