The error `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'ɵcmp')` typically occurs when Angular cannot find the component's metadata, which might be due to how standalone components are declared and imported in your library.

Ensure that all your components within the library are correctly marked as standalone. For instance, your `CardComponent` should look like this:

```typescript
import { Component, Input, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

@Component({
  selector: 'card',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule],
  template: `
    <div class="{{classes}}" style="width: {{width}}; border-radius: {{radius}}">
      <!-- Your component content here -->
    </div>
  `,
  styles: []
})
export class CardComponent implements OnInit {
  @Input() classes: string = '';
  @Input() width: string = '';
  @Input() radius: string = 'sm';

  constructor() { }

  ngOnInit(): void {
  }
}
```

In your app, you should import the component directly without declaring it in a module:

```typescript
import { CardComponent } from 'path-to-your-library/card.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <card [classes]="'bg-blue-500'" [width]="'200px'" [radius]="'md'"></card>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {}
```

Make sure your `public-api.ts` correctly exports all components. If you're still facing issues, ensure that the library is built and the paths are correct in your app's `package.json`.

