In Angular 16, `entryComponents` are no longer used in `AppModule`. Instead, you should declare these components in the `declarations` array of `AppModule`, and ensure they are also included in the `entryComponents` array if they need to be bootstrapped dynamically. This setup allows Angular to know about both the existence and the dynamic loading capabilities of these components.

Here’s how you can adjust your `app.module.ts`:

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { PayResultDialogComponent, MessageBoxComponent } from './path-to-components';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent,
    PayResultDialogComponent,
    MessageBoxComponent
  ],
  entryComponents: [PayResultDialogComponent, MessageBoxComponent],
  // other configurations...
})
export class AppModule {}
```

By adding `PayResultDialogComponent` and `MessageBoxComponent` to the `declarations` array, you ensure they are part of your application. Adding them to `entryComponents` allows these components to be dynamically loaded when needed, such as through dialog services or lazy loading modules.

