To correctly identify whether the passed parameter is a sequential or associative array in Laravel, you can use the `Arr` facade from the Illuminate\Support package. Specifically, you should check if the array is associative using `Arr::isAssoc()` and ensure it's not detected as a list (sequential) by `Arr::isList()`. Here’s how you can modify your method:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Arr;

public function doSomething($passedArray) {
    if (is_array($passedArray)) {
        if (Arr::isList($passedArray)) {
            echo "The passed array is a sequential array.\n";
        } elseif (Arr::isAssoc($passedArray)) {
            echo "The passed array is an associative array with non-integer keys.\n";
        } else {
            echo "The passed array is an associative array with integer keys.\n";
        }
    } else {
        echo "The passed variable is not an array.\n";
    }
}
```

This approach ensures that `$associative_sequential` and `$sequential` are correctly identified as sequential arrays, while the other cases are properly classified.

