public function test_something_with_reCaptcha(): void
{
    $secret = config('services.recaptcha.secret');

    $mock = Mockery::mock(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, function (MockInterface $mock) use ($secret) {
        $mock->shouldReceive('make')->once()->andReturn($mock);
        $mock->shouldReceive('verify')->once()->andReturn(true); // Adjust the return value as needed
    });

    $this->instance(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, $mock);

    $recaptcha = resolve(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, ['secret' => $secret]);

    // Your test logic here...
}
```

This approach leverages `resolve` to instantiate the class with its required parameters and mock it using Mockery. This keeps your code clean and reduces unnecessary complexity.

