The confusion arises from misunderstanding how `@for` works within Angular templates. When you use `@for`, the element itself and its children are repeated, not just the children as in older Angular syntax with `*ngFor`. Therefore, you can directly include `[ngClass]` or any other directive on the element being iterated over.

Here’s how you can refactor your code:

```html
@for (dt of totals; track $index) {
  <div [ngClass]="{'highlight': dt > 10}">
    {{ dt }}
  </div>
}
```

In this example, `div` is the element being repeated for each item in `totals`. The `[ngClass]` directive works directly on `div`, and it correctly applies the class based on the condition. This approach avoids the need for an extra `ng-container`.

Remember, with `@for`, you can apply any attribute or directive to the element itself, not just its children.

