Lazy loading still works with Angular v17's default standalone approach. You can define routes for lazy-loaded components directly without needing `@NgModule`. For example, your route configuration will work as intended:

```typescript
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: '', component: DashboardComponent },
  { path: 'users', component: UsersComponent },
  { path: '**', component: DashboardComponent }
];
```

However, for lazy loading to be effective, ensure the components are standalone. If a component is part of an `@NgModule`, it won't be lazily loaded even if you use `loadChildren`. For instance:

- **Standalone Component**: Works with lazy loading.
  ```typescript
  @Component({ selector: 'app-users', template: '<p>Users</p>' })
  export class UsersComponent {}
  ```

- **Non-Standalone Component (part of NgModule)**: Does not work for lazy loading.
  ```typescript
  @Component({ selector: 'app-users', template: '<p>Users</p>' })
  export class UsersComponent {}

  @NgModule({
    declarations: [UsersComponent],
    imports: [RouterModule.forChild([{ path: '', component: UsersComponent }])]
  })
  export class UsersModule {}
  ```

For lazy loading, you can use `loadChildren` as shown in the documentation:

```typescript
const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'users',
    loadChildren: () => import('./users/users.module').then(m => m.UsersModule)
  }
];
```

This setup leverages Angular's new default while maintaining lazy loading capabilities.

