The issue you're encountering with contextual information not being merged when specifying a custom log channel in Laravel v10 might be due to how the `withContext` method is used or not used properly. When logging to a specific channel, ensure that any context added globally via `Log::withContext()` is also applied to the specific channel.

Here’s an approach to ensure contextual information works across all channels:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;
use Illuminate\Support\Str;

// Add global context for request ID
Log::withContext([
    'request-id' => (string) Str::uuid()
]);

// Log with a specific channel, ensuring the global context is applied
Log::channel('mychannel')->info(
    'User {user} created Thing {id}',
    [
        'user' => auth()->user()->id,
        'id' => $thing->id
    ]
);
```

Alternatively, you can manually add the context to each log entry when specifying a channel:

```php
Log::channel('mychannel')->withContext([
    'request-id' => (string) Str::uuid()
])->info(
    'User {user} created Thing {id}',
    [
        'user' => auth()->user()->id,
        'id' => $thing->id
    ]
);
```

This ensures that the `request-id` is included in logs written to the `mychannel`, even when specifying a channel directly.

