To return a 404 error page with the appropriate HTTP status code from your middleware, you need to use Laravel's `response()->view()` method instead of returning plain text. Here’s how you can modify your `handle` function:

```php
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
    if ($request->user() === null || $request->user()->role !== 'admin') {
        return response()->view('errors.404', [], 404);
    }

    return $next($request);
}
```

This code checks if the user is not authenticated or not an admin. If either condition is true, it returns a 404 error page by rendering the `errors.404` view with the HTTP status code set to 404.

Ensure you have the appropriate 404 view defined in your `resources/views/errors/404.blade.php`. This approach will properly return a custom 404 error page instead of plain text, making it appear as if the routes don't exist when they should be restricted.

