The issue arises from how Angular handles tracking expressions in `@for` loops with components. When you use a component inside an `@for` loop, each instance of that component shares the same input property due to the way tracking works by default. This means changes to one instance affect others.

To fix this, modify your `app.component.ts` to track by unique identifiers instead of indices:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <ul>
      @for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
        <li><app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle(todo)"></app-slider></li>
      }
    </ul>
  `,
  imports: [SliderComponent]
})
export class App {
  // ... other code ...

  toggle(todo: Todo) {
    todo.done = !todo.done;
    this.buildTodos();
  }
}
```

By tracking by `todo.id`, each instance of `SliderComponent` gets its own unique input, preventing state changes from affecting others.

