To achieve lazy loading for each iframe in your video gallery, you can use Angular's `@defer` directive with appropriate triggers. Since you want iframes to load as the page scrolls or one by one, you should specify a trigger that responds to these events.

Here’s how you can modify your code:

```angular-ts
@Component({
  ...
  template: `
    @for (link of youtubeLinks; track $index) {
      @defer (on viewport) {
        <iframe [src]="link" frameborder="0"></iframe>
      }
    }
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  youtubeLinks = ['https://www.youtube.com/embed/...']; // Your video links
}
```

In this example, `@defer (on viewport)` ensures that each iframe is loaded only when the corresponding section of the page becomes visible. This approach can significantly improve your application's performance and user experience by reducing initial load times.

If you want to control loading more precisely, such as loading one iframe at a time, consider using custom logic with `@defer (when condition)` or combining multiple triggers like `on viewport; on timer(2s)`. However, for simple lazy loading based on scroll events, the above approach should suffice.

