The behavior you're observing stems from how Angular handles references and copies in different contexts. When using `@for` (a template-based for loop), each iteration creates a new instance of the component, which means any changes to properties within that instance do not affect the original object passed from the parent.

In contrast, when using `*ngFor`, Angular reuses instances if possible, ensuring that changes made in one instance reflect in the original data model. This is because `*ngFor` maintains a reference to the same objects as those in your array.

To achieve consistent behavior with `@for`, you can use two-way binding or an event emitter to notify the parent component of changes and update the original object there. For example, modify your child component like this:

```typescript
// ChildComponent
import { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: `
    <button (click)="toggle()">Toggle</button>
  `,
})
export class ChildComponent {
  @Input() activeProblem!: ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem;
  problemGroup?: ProblemGroup;
  narrativeProblem?: NarrativeProblem;

  ngOnInit(): void {
    if (this.activeProblem instanceof ProblemGroup) {
      this.problemGroup = this.activeProblem as ProblemGroup;
    } else {
      this.narrativeProblem = this.activeProblem;
    }
  }

  toggle() {
    // Logic to toggle the problem group
    if (this.problemGroup) {
      // Example mutation
      this.problemGroup.isActive = !this.problemGroup.isActive;
    }
    // Emit an event to notify the parent of changes
    this.onProblemChanged.emit(this.activeProblem);
  }
}
```

In your parent component, handle the emitted event:

```typescript
// AppComponent
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <main *ngFor="let problem of problems; let i = index">
      <app-child [activeProblem]="problems[i]"></app-child>
    </main>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  // Assume `problems` is an array of ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem
  onProblemChanged(problem: ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem) {
    const index = this.problems.indexOf(problem);
    if (index !== -1) {
      this.problems[index] = problem; // Update the original object
    }
  }
}
```

This approach ensures that changes in the child component are reflected in the parent

