The issue arises because `ActivatedRoute` needs to be provided in the module or component that uses it, not just imported. Since you're using standalone components and don't have an AppModule, ensure `RouterLink`, `RouterOutlet`, and `RouterModule` are correctly configured at the app level.

In your `app.config.ts`, add `RouterModule.forRoot(routes)`:

```ts
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideRouter, RouterModule } from '@angular/router';

import { routes } from './app.routes';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideRouter(routes), RouterModule.forRoot(routes)]
};
```

Also, ensure your `AppComponent` imports and uses `RouterLink`, `RouterOutlet`, and `RouterModule` correctly:

```ts
import { Component, LOCALE_ID } from '@angular/core';
import { registerLocaleData } from '@angular/common';
import { RouterLink, RouterOutlet } from '@angular/router';
import { SharedModule } from './shared/shared.module';
import localePT from '@angular/common/locales/pt';

registerLocaleData(localePT);

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [SharedModule, RouterOutlet, RouterLink],
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css'],
  providers: [{provide: LOCALE_ID, useValue: 'pt-br'}]
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = "app";
}
```

And in your `app.routes.ts`, define the routes as shown:

```ts
import { Routes } from '@angular/router';
import { HomeComponent } from './features/pages/home/home.component';

export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'home', component: HomeComponent },
  { path: '', redirectTo: 'home', pathMatch: 'full' }
];
```

This setup ensures that `RouterModule` is properly configured and all necessary providers are available.

