The issue you're encountering likely stems from how Angular handles route changes and initial rendering during page reloads. When you navigate or reload a protected route, your guard checks if the user is authenticated. If not, it redirects to the login page. However, due to the asynchronous nature of Observables in RxJS (used by `pipe(map))`, there might be a brief moment where the component tree is rendered before the guard's logic fully executes.

To address this, you can ensure that your guard returns immediately if the user isn't authenticated, preventing any rendering until the check completes. Here’s an adjusted version of your AuthGuard:

```typescript
export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) => {
  const tokenService = inject(TokenService);
  return tokenService.isAuthentications.pipe(
    map(user => !user ? router.createUrlTree(['/login']) : true)
  );
};
```

Additionally, ensure your `TokenService` correctly handles the initial check:

```typescript
export class TokenService {
  isAuthentications: BehaviorSubject<boolean> = new BehaviorSubject(false);

  constructor(@Inject(PLATFORM_ID) private platformId: Object) { 
    const token = this.getToken();
    if (token) {
      this.updateToken(true);
    }
  }

  setToken(token: string){
    this.updateToken(true);
    localStorage.setItem('user', token);
  }

  updateToken(status: boolean){
    this.isAuthentications.next(status);
  }

  getToken(): string | null{
    return localStorage.getItem('user');
  }
}
```

By ensuring the guard returns a `UrlTree` or `true` immediately, you prevent any unnecessary rendering. This approach should help mitigate the brief flash of the login page during reloads.

