The issue you're encountering arises from how Angular interprets the `@for` and `@if` blocks in your template. The `@for` block does not directly support conditional rendering like `@if`. Instead, you can use an `@empty` block to handle cases where there is no data.

To achieve your goal of showing a table or a message when there's no data, you should structure your template as follows:

```angular-html
@for (order of licenseOverview.orders; track order.id) {
  {{ order.reference }}
  {{ ... }}
}
@empty {
  No data for you!
}
```

This will display the orders using `@for` and show "No data for you!" when there are no orders. Note that `$first` and `$last` are not directly usable in this context; instead, rely on the presence or absence of items to conditionally render content.

For more complex scenarios involving headers and footers, consider using a component or a custom directive to encapsulate your logic, ensuring Angular can properly parse and render your template.

