Your approach is on the right track but can be improved for maintainability and alignment with Laravel conventions. Instead of including migration files directly, you can use the `Migration` class to reference your migrations by name. Here’s a cleaner way:

1. **Define an Artisan Command**:
   ```php
   namespace App\Console\Commands;

   use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
   use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
   use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
   use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;
   use Illuminate\Console\Command;

   class RefreshMyTables extends Command
   {
       protected $signature = 'refresh-my-tables';

       public function handle()
       {
           // Define the migration classes by name
           $migrations = [
               \App\database\Migrations\CreateMyTableNumbersTable::class,
               \App\database\Migrations\CreateMyTableFilesTable::class,
               \App\database\Migrations\CreateMyTableOrdersTable::class,
               \App\database\Migrations\CreateMyTableParentTable::class
           ];

           // Rollback and then migrate in reverse order to handle dependencies
           foreach ($migrations as $migration) {
               (new $migration)->down();
           }

           foreach (array_reverse($migrations) as $migration) {
               (new $migration)->up();
           }

           // Seed the database
           (new \App\Database\Seeders\MyTableSeeder())->run();
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Register the Command**:
   Ensure your command is registered in `app/Console/Kernel.php`.

This approach leverages Laravel's PSR-4 autoloading to dynamically load migration classes, making it more maintainable and less prone to errors related to file paths.

