To retrieve the required parameter in your controller method, you don't need to use `Request $request` explicitly if the route has already bound the parameter to a model or primitive value. In your example where the route is `/route/{param}`, Laravel will automatically pass the `{param}` value as an argument to your controller method.

Here's how you can define and handle this in your controller:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Controller;

class ExampleController extends Controller
{
    public function some_method(string $param)
    {
        // Now, `$param` contains '123' if the URL is /route/123.
        echo $param;
    }
}
```

In this case, you don't need to inject `Request $request` because Laravel automatically passes the route parameter as an argument. If your route definition looks like:

```php
Route::get('/route/{param}', [ExampleController::class, 'some_method']);
```

The value of `{param}` will be passed directly to the `some_method` method.

If you need additional information from the request (like headers or other data) alongside the route parameter, then you can still use `Request $request`. However, for just getting a single route parameter, this approach is sufficient.

