In Angular 16, `entryComponents` are no longer added directly in `AppModule`. Instead, you should declare these components within their respective feature modules or in a shared module if they will be reused across multiple feature modules. This approach aligns with best practices for managing components.

For example, to include `PayResultDialogComponent` and `MessageBoxComponent`, you can add them to the `declarations` array of the relevant feature module:

```typescript
// src/app/customer-dashboard/customer-dashboard.module.ts

import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

import { CustomerDashboardComponent } from './customer-dashboard/customer-dashboard.component';
import { PayResultDialogComponent, MessageBoxComponent } from './path-to-components'; // Adjust the import paths as necessary

@NgModule({
  imports: [
    CommonModule
  ],
  declarations: [
    CustomerDashboardComponent,
    PayResultDialogComponent,
    MessageBoxComponent
  ]
})
export class CustomerDashboardModule { }
```

This ensures that these components are properly declared and can be used within their respective modules. If the components need to be shared across multiple feature modules, consider creating a `SharedModule` as described in Documentation Excerpt [5].

