Yes, you can load a standalone component in Angular 16 as a dialog without using routing or preloading. Since your component is standalone, you don't need to configure any routes for lazy loading. Here’s how you can do it:

First, ensure your component is marked as `standalone` and has the necessary imports:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dialog',
  template: `<div>Dialog Content</div>`
})
export class DialogComponent {}
```

To open this dialog, you can use a service or directly in your component. Here’s an example of opening the dialog using a service:

1. Create a service to handle the dialog:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { ComponentType } from '@angular/cdk/portal';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class DialogService {
  openDialog<T>(componentType: ComponentType<T>, data?: any): void {
    // Implementation to open the dialog, e.g., using a modal library
  }
}
```

2. Use this service in your component to open the dialog:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { DialogService } from './dialog.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-your-component',
  template: `<button (click)="openDialog()">Open Dialog</button>`
})
export class YourComponent {
  constructor(private dialogService: DialogService) {}

  openDialog(): void {
    this.dialogService.openDialog(DialogComponent, { /* pass data if needed */ });
  }
}
```

This approach avoids the need for routing or preloading and directly opens your standalone component as a dialog.

