In Laravel, you can specify the controller's namespace either by using the `->namespace()` method in your route definition or by explicitly importing the fully qualified class name at the top of your file. Both methods are correct but serve slightly different purposes and contexts.

For clarity and to avoid potential issues with autoloading, it is recommended to use the explicit import statement:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PhotoController;

Route::prefix('admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

This approach makes your intentions clear and ensures that Laravel can correctly resolve the class. However, if you prefer to manage namespaces within route definitions for organizational purposes, you can use:

```php
Route::prefix('admin')->namespace('App\Http\Controllers\Admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

This method is useful when defining multiple routes that share the same namespace. Both methods should resolve the "Class 'PhotoController' does not exist" error, but using `use` statements can make your code more readable and maintainable.

