To accurately identify whether an array is associative or not in Laravel, you can use the `Arr::isAssoc` method from the Illuminate\Support\Arr facade. This method checks if the keys of the array are non-sequential and non-integer based, which aligns with your requirements.

Here's how you can modify your function:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Arr;

public function doSomething($passedArray) {
    if (is_array($passedArray)) {
        if ($passedArray == array_values($passedArray)) {
            echo "The passed array is a sequential array.\n";
        } elseif (Arr::isAssoc($passedArray)) {
            echo "The passed array is an associative array with non-integer keys.\n";
        } else {
            echo "The passed array is an associative array with integer keys.\n";
        }
    } else {
        echo "The passed variable is not an array.\n";
    }
}
```

This approach ensures that arrays with sequential, integer-based keys are correctly identified as non-associative. The `Arr::isAssoc` method from the Laravel framework will help you distinguish between associative and non-associative arrays accurately.

