To unit test an external mocked class with constructor parameters in Laravel without creating a factory, you can use the `resolve` method provided by Laravel's service container to pass the required constructor arguments directly. This approach simplifies your code and reduces unnecessary complexity.

Here’s how you can do it:

```php tab=Pest
use App\Http\Controllers\YourController;
use ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha;
use Mockery\MockInterface;

test('verify recaptcha in controller', function () {
    $secret = config('services.recaptcha.secret');

    $recaptcha = resolve(ReCaptcha::class, ['secret' => $secret]);

    // Mock the recaptcha instance
    $mock = Mockery::mock(ReCaptcha::class)
        ->shouldReceive('make')
        ->once()
        ->andReturn($mock)
        ->shouldReceive('verify')
        ->once()
        ->andReturn(true);

    $this->instance(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, $mock);

    // Call the controller method
    $response = (new YourController)->yourMethod();

    // Assert your expectations
});
```

This approach uses `resolve` to pass the required constructor parameter and mocks the instance directly in the test. This way, you avoid creating a factory and keep your tests clean and focused on the behavior being tested.

