To include Angular services with lazy-loaded standalone components, you can use the `providedIn` token in your service decorator and set it to `'root'`. However, since you're using standalone components, this approach won't work directly. Instead, you should create a feature module for each group of related components that require shared services.

For example, if `DashboardComponent` and `SettingsComponent` share common services, create a new feature module:

```shell
ng generate module authorized --route authorized --module app.module
```

Then, move the relevant components into this module. Update your lazy-loaded route to load this module:

**app.routes.ts:**
```typescript
export const appRoutes: Routes = [
  { path: '', redirectTo: '/dashboard', pathMatch: 'full' },
  {
    path: 'login',
    component: LoginComponent,
    title: 'Login',
  },
  {
    path: '',
    canActivateChild: [AuthGuard],
    loadChildren: () => import(`./authorized/authorized.module`).then((m) => m.AuthorizedModule),
  },
  { path: '**', redirectTo: '/dashboard' },
];
```

**authorized.routes.ts:**
```typescript
export const authorizedRoutes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'dashboard',
    component: DashboardComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Dashboard',
  },
  {
    path: 'settings',
    component: SettingsComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Settings',
  },
];
```

In this setup, the `authorized.module.ts` can declare and provide services that are shared among its components. This ensures these services are only loaded when the user navigates to authorized routes after logging in.

Remember to adjust your service providers as needed:

```typescript
@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'module', // or a specific feature module name if you have multiple modules
})
export class SharedService {}
```

This approach leverages Angular's lazy loading and module system effectively, even when using standalone components.

