The official way to refactor `ngFor` with an element using the iteration variable and applying `ngClass` can be done by including the `dt` in the template directly, without needing an extra `ng-container`. The key insight is that `*ngFor` repeats both the element itself and its children. Here’s how you can do it:

```html
@for (dt of totals; track $index) {
  <div [ngClass]="{'highlight': dt > 10}">
    {{ dt }}
  </div>
}
```

In this example, `dt` is available within the template block and can be used directly with attributes like `[ngClass]`. This approach avoids adding unnecessary `ng-container` elements while correctly utilizing the iteration variable.

