To return a 404 view with the appropriate HTTP status code from your middleware, you need to use Laravel's `response()->view()` method instead of returning plain text. Here’s how you can modify your `handle` function:

```php
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
    if ($request->user() === null || $request->user()->role !== 'admin') {
        return response()->view('errors.404', [], 404);
    }

    return $next($request);
}
```

This code checks if the user is not authenticated or does not have the admin role, then returns a 404 status and renders the `errors.404` view. This approach ensures that when the conditions are not met, users see the appropriate 404 error page instead of any other content.

Make sure you have an `errors.404.blade.php` file in your `resources/views/errors` directory to define what this 404 view looks like.

