To display items from index 1 of an array of strings in Angular, you can use the `$index` variable provided by the `@for` loop to skip the first item. Here's how:

```angular-html
@for (item of arr; track $index) {
  <p *ngIf="$index > 0"> {{ item }} </p>
}
```

This code uses Angular's `*ngIf` directive to conditionally render each item, excluding the first one by checking if `$index` is greater than 0. The `track $index` ensures efficient updates when items are added or removed from the array.

Alternatively, you can slice the array in your component logic before passing it to the template:

```typescript
arr = [ "str1", "str2", "str3", "str4", "str5" ];
filteredArr = this.arr.slice(1); // Exclude the first item

@Component({
  ...
  template: `
    @for (item of filteredArr; track $index) {
      <p> {{ item }} </p>
    }
  `,
})
export class AppComponent { }
```

This approach keeps your template cleaner by handling data transformation in the component logic.

