To use `withFetch()` with an application that is not bootstrapped as a standalone app, you should configure `HttpClient` in your root module (e.g., `AppModule`). Place the following configuration in your `AppModule`:

```typescript
import { provideHttpClient, withFetch } from '@angular/common/http';

@NgModule({
  // ... other configurations
})
export class AppModule {
  constructor() {
    import('zone.js').then(() => {
      provideHttpClient(withFetch());
    });
  }
}
```

This ensures that `fetch` is used for HTTP requests in your application. Placing this configuration in `app.server.module.ts` as you did for server-side rendering (SSR) is also a valid approach, but it should be done in the root module if you want to ensure consistent behavior across both client and server environments.

By configuring `withFetch()` in your root module (`AppModule`), you adhere to best practices for applications that are not bootstrapped as standalone components.

