The issue arises from how Angular tracks changes in the `@for` loop using the tracking expression. In your case, both the `undoneTodos` and `doneTodos` arrays are being rebuilt entirely with each toggle operation due to the lack of an effective tracking strategy. This results in a full re-rendering of all items within these lists.

To fix this, you should use a unique identifier for tracking instead of `$index`. Update your template as follows:

```html
@for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
  <app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle(todo)"></app-slider>
}
```

And similarly for `doneTodos`.

This change ensures that only the specific item's state is updated, rather than re-rendering all items. The tracking expression `track todo.id` helps Angular identify which items have actually changed and need to be updated, avoiding unnecessary DOM re-creations.

Remember, using `$index` as a tracking token can lead to issues when items are added or removed from the array, as it doesn't uniquely identify each item.

