The behavior you're observing stems from how Angular handles references and copies in templates. When using `@for` with an input binding like `[(activeProblem)]="problem"`, Angular may create a copy of the object to ensure immutability, which can lead to unexpected results when modifying the object inside the child component.

In contrast, `*ngFor` typically works with references by default, meaning that changes made within the child component directly affect the parent's data. This is why mutations in the child component are reflected back to the original object when using `*ngFor`.

To ensure consistent behavior and avoid unintended copies, you can use a custom pipe or service to manage the reference passing between components. Alternatively, consider using an observable for two-way binding if you need more control over how data is shared.

Here’s a simplified example of using a custom pipe:

```typescript
// problem.pipe.ts
import { Pipe, PipeTransform } from '@angular/core';

@Pipe({ name: 'problem' })
export class ProblemPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(value: any): any {
    return value; // Ensure no copy is made
  }
}
```

Then in your template:

```html
<app-child [activeProblem]="problem | problem"></app-child>
```

This approach helps maintain the reference and ensures that changes are reflected correctly.

