In Angular 17, you can use `@if` and `@else` to conditionally display content without combining statements or using logical OR (`||`). Here’s how you can achieve the desired behavior:

```angular-html
@if (movies) {
  @if (movies.length > 0) {
    Favorites

    Recent 
    {{ movies[movies.length - 1].title }}
    -
    {{ movies[movies.length - 1].rating }}

    Total 
    {{ movies.length }}
  } @else {
    @else {
      Favorites

      Recent 
      NA

      Total 
      0
    }
  }
}
```

This code checks if `movies` is truthy. If it has items, it displays the recent movie details and total count. Otherwise, it shows "NA" for recent and a total of 0.

Note that Angular's template syntax allows nesting `@if` and `@else` blocks to handle different conditions appropriately without combining them into one statement.

