To resolve the "Object is possibly 'undefined'" error in your template, you can use Angular's `*ngIf` directive to ensure that the posts array is defined before attempting to access its elements. This approach also improves readability and maintains type safety.

Modify your template as follows:

```html
Dashboard

@for(post of posts; track post.title) {
  @if(post && post.id % 2 == 0) {
    {{post.id}}-{{post.title}}
  }
}
```

By adding `@if(post && post.id % 2 == 0)`, you ensure that the template only processes elements where both `post` and `post.id` are defined, thus avoiding the undefined error.

Alternatively, if you want to keep your original structure, you can use a non-null assertion operator (`!`) but be cautious as it suppresses errors without addressing the underlying issue:

```html
@for(post of posts; track post.title) {
  @if(post.id % 2 == 0) {
    {{post!.id}}-{{post!.title}}
  }
}
```

However, using `*ngIf` is generally recommended to maintain type safety and avoid potential runtime errors.

