To achieve the desired output in Laravel using Eloquent, you can use the `sum` method on collections as shown in the documentation excerpt [1]. However, since your data is an array of objects, first convert it to a collection and then sum up the quantities based on the barcode. Here's how:

```php
$items = [
    ['id' => 1, 'barcode' => '555', 'description' => 'pencil', 'quantity' => 3],
    ['id' => 2, 'barcode' => '555', 'description' => 'pencil', 'quantity' => 1],
    // ... more items
];

$collection = collect($items);

$result = $collection->groupBy('barcode')
                     ->map(function ($group) {
                         return [
                             'id' => $group->first()->id,
                             'barcode' => $group->first()->barcode,
                             'description' => $group->first()->description,
                             'qty' => $group->sum('quantity'),
                         ];
                     })->all();

// Output: Array of objects with summed quantities
```

This code groups the items by barcode, sums up the quantities for each group, and then constructs a new array of objects with the desired structure.

