In your scenario, you don't necessarily need to convert the signal to an observable just for side effects. Signals automatically trigger their side effects whenever their value changes, without requiring a subscription or `pipe/subscribe` pattern. 

Here’s how you can implement the side effect directly with a signal:

```ts
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({...})
export class MyComponent {
  isItEditor = signal(false); // Signal to track editor state

  constructor(private formGroup: FormGroup) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.isItEditor.set(true); // Example initial value
    this.isItEditor.on((value) => {
      const funded = this.formGroup.controls.funded;
      if (value)
        funded.enable();
      else
        funded.disable();
    });
  }
}
```

In this example, setting the `isItEditor` signal directly triggers the side effect of enabling or disabling the `funded` control. This approach is more concise and leverages signals' built-in behavior for handling value changes.

