The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular handles signals and change detection outside of the Angular zone. When you disable the Angular zone with `ngZone: 'noop'` in your `main.ts`, Angular's default change detection mechanism is bypassed, which means it won't automatically trigger a view update even if you call `signal.update()`.

To ensure that changes to signals are reflected in the view when outside of the Angular zone, you need to manually run change detection. You can do this by re-entering the Angular zone using `NgZone.run()`. Here’s how you can modify your code:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { signal } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    {{ title() }} 
    Change 
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually run change detection
    this.ngZone.run(() => {});
  }

  constructor(private ngZone: NgZone) {}
}
```

By wrapping the call to `title.update()` inside `this.ngZone.run()`, you ensure that Angular's change detection runs and updates the view accordingly.

