To remove `public` from your URLs without moving files, you need to adjust your Nginx configuration rather than using `.htaccess`. The provided `.htaccess` rules are for Apache and won't work with Nginx. Here’s how you can modify your Nginx server block:

1. Ensure all requests are directed to `index.php`.
2. Adjust the root directory path.

Here's an example of what your Nginx configuration might look like:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name domain.com;

    root /path/to/your/project/cms/public; # Adjust this to point to your project's public folder

    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN";
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff";

    index index.php;

    charset utf-8;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.php?$query_string;
    }

    location = /favicon.ico { access_log off; log_not_found off; }
    location = /robots.txt  { access_log off; log_not_found off; }

    error_page 404 /index.php;

    location ~ \.php$ {
        fastcgi_pass unix:/var/run/php/php8.2-fpm.sock;
        fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $realpath_root$fastcgi_script_name;
        include fastcgi_params;
    }
}
```

