In Laravel, you can directly access route parameters in your controller methods without needing to use the `Request` object. When defining a route with a parameter, like `/users/{user}`, Laravel automatically passes that parameter as an argument to the corresponding controller method.

For example, if you have the following route:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\UserController;

Route::get('/users/{id}', [UserController::class, 'show']);
```

And your `UserController` looks like this:

```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\View\View;

class UserController extends Controller
{
    /**
     * Show the profile for a given user.
     */
    public function show(string $id): View
    {
        return view('user.profile', [
            'user' => User::findOrFail($id)
        ]);
    }
}
```

The `$id` parameter from the route is automatically passed to the `show` method. You can use it directly in your method signature.

If you want to access a route parameter using the global helper function, you can do so like this:

```php
public function show()
{
    $id = request()->route('id');
    // Use $id as needed...
}
```

However, for most cases where you have defined route-model-binding or are working with simple integer IDs, directly using the parameter in your method signature is more straightforward and efficient.

