To allow users to download files from both views in Laravel 10, you can create a `download` method within your `UploadController`. This method will handle downloading the file based on the upload ID passed via URL parameters.

First, define the route for the download action:

```php
Route::get('/uploads/download/{upload}', [UploadController::class, 'download'])->name('uploads.download');
```

Next, implement the `download` method in your `UploadController` to handle file downloads:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Storage;

public function download(Upload $upload)
{
    return Storage::disk($upload->disk)->download($upload->path);
}
```

In both your index and show views, you can add a link to this route using the upload's ID:

Index view:
```blade
@foreach ($uploads as $upload)
    <a href="{{ route('uploads.download', ['upload' => $upload->id]) }}">Download {{ $upload->name }}</a>
@endforeach
```

Show view:
```blade
<a href="{{ route('uploads.download', ['upload' => $upload->id]) }}">Download</a>
```

This approach keeps your code clean and leverages Laravel's routing and file handling capabilities. The `download` method uses the `Storage` facade to serve the file from its storage location, making it easy for users to download any upload directly from both views.

