In Laravel, you can specify the controller's namespace either by using the `->namespace()` method in your route definition or by explicitly importing the fully qualified class name at the top of your routes file. Both methods are correct but serve slightly different purposes and contexts.

To use the `->namespace()` method:
```php
Route::prefix('admin')->namespace('App\Http\Controllers\Admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```
This is useful when you want to group multiple routes under a specific namespace without importing each controller individually.

To import the fully qualified class name:
```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PhotoController;

Route::prefix('admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```
This is more explicit and can be useful for clarity, especially in larger route files.

For your scenario, using `->namespace()` is a cleaner approach since you're already grouping routes under the 'Admin' namespace. Ensure that the controller class name matches exactly with the one defined in `PhotoController::class`.

In summary, both methods are correct; choose based on readability and maintainability preferences for your project.

