To include Angular services with your lazy-loaded standalone component routes, you can use the `providedIn` token in the service decorator and set it to `'root'`. However, since you're using standalone components, a better approach is to create a shared module that provides these services. This way, when you load this module lazily, the services will be available only where needed.

1. Create a shared module for your services:
```typescript
// shared.module.ts
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

@NgModule({
  imports: [CommonModule],
  providers: [
    // List your services here
    YourService,
    AnotherService
  ]
})
export class SharedModule {}
```

2. Modify `appAuthorizedRoutes` to include the shared module:
```typescript
// app-authorized.routes.ts
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';
import { SharedModule } from './shared.module';

const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'dashboard',
    component: DashboardComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Dashboard'
  },
  {
    path: 'settings',
    component: SettingsComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Settings'
  },
  // etc...
];

@NgModule({
  imports: [
    RouterModule.forChild(routes),
    SharedModule
  ]
})
export class AppAuthorizedRoutesModule {}
```

3. Update your `app.routes.ts` to lazy-load the new module:
```typescript
// app.routes.ts
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';

const routes: Routes = [
  // ...
  {
    path: '',
    canActivateChild: [AuthGuard],
    loadChildren: () => import('./app-authorized.routes').then(m => m.AppAuthorizedRoutesModule)
  },
  // ...
];

@NgModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes)],
  exports: [RouterModule]
})
export class AppRoutingModule {}
```

This setup ensures that the services are only available when the `AppAuthorizedRoutesModule` is loaded, which happens after the user logs in.

