The issue lies in how you're handling the response type and converting JSON data to your custom `AddEmployee` class. Here’s a concise solution:

1. Ensure your service returns the correct `responseType`.

2. Convert the JSON data to an array of `AddEmployee` objects.

3. Use `map()` in your service to transform the data before assigning it to `employees`.

Here's how you can adjust your code:

**EmployeeService Service:**
```typescript
@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class EmployeeServiceService {

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }

  getAllEmployees(): Observable<AddEmployee[]> {
    console.log("Inside get ALL Employees Method.");
    return this.http.get<any>("https://localhost:9090/employee/getAllEmployees", { responseType: 'json' })
      .pipe(map(data => data.map(item => new AddEmployee(
        item.firstName,
        item.lastName,
        item.address,
        item.project,
        item.ssn,
        new Date(item.joinDate),
        item.status
      ))));
  }
}
```

**Employee Component:**
```typescript
employees: AddEmployee[];

constructor(private service: EmployeeServiceService) { }

ngOnInit() {
  this.getAllEmployees1();
}

public getAllEmployees1() {
  this.service.getAllEmployees().subscribe(
    (data) => {
      this.employees = data;
      console.log("Response: ", this.employees);
    },
    (error) => {
      console.error('Error fetching employees: ', error);
    }
  );
}
```

**Employee.Component.html:**
```html
<div *ngIf="employees">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>First Name</th>
        <th>Last Name</th>
        <!-- Add more headers as needed -->
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr *ngFor="let employee of employees">
        <td>{{employee.firstName}}</td>
        <td>{{employee.lastName}}</td>
        <!-- Add more columns as needed -->
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
```

This setup ensures the JSON data is correctly mapped to `AddEmployee` objects, and you can use `*ngFor` in your template.

