To set up locale-based routing in your Laravel Inertia application, you can use route parameters and middleware effectively. Your current approach with `SetLocaleMiddleware` is on the right track but needs some adjustments to correctly capture the locale from the URL parameter.

First, ensure your routes are defined as follows:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;

Route::middleware(['setLocale'])->prefix('{locale?}')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('posts', PostController::class);
    Route::resource('comments', CommentController::class);
});
```

Next, modify `SetLocaleMiddleware` to correctly capture the locale from the route parameter:

```php
namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class SetLocaleMiddleware
{
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next)
    {
        $locale = $request->route('locale') ?? 'en'; // Default to 'en' if not provided

        \Log::info($locale); // Logs the correct locale or 'en'

        App::setLocale($locale);

        return $next($request);
    }
}
```

Ensure your `app/Http/Kernel.php` includes this middleware alias:

```php
protected $middlewareAliases = [
    'setLocale' => \App\Http\Middleware\SetLocaleMiddleware::class,
];
```

This setup ensures that the locale is correctly set based on the URL parameter, with a default of `'en'`. For Inertia to work seamlessly, make sure your Vue components are properly using the `@lang` directive or equivalent from `laravel-vue-i18n`.

Remember, the key is in capturing the locale from the route and setting it before handling the request. This approach should align with your expectations for locale-based routing.

