To handle `ReferenceError: window is not defined` in your Angular 17 SSR application with ApexCharts, you can use the `afterNextRender()` lifecycle hook to ensure your chart initialization runs only on the client side. Here’s how you can adjust your code:

```typescript
import { Component, ElementRef, afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';
import ApexCharts from 'apexcharts';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  templateUrl: './dashboard.component.html'
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  @ViewChild('chart') chart!: ElementRef;

  constructor() {
    // Use `afterNextRender` to ensure the chart is initialized only on the client side
    afterNextRender(() => {
      const element = this.chart.nativeElement;
      if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && element) { // Check for browser environment
        new ApexCharts(element, {
          series: [
            { name: 'STOCK ABC', data: [/* your data */] },
          ],
          chart: { type: 'line' }, // Add other options as needed
        }).render();
      }
    });
  }

  ngOnInit(): void {}
}
```

This approach ensures that the `ApexCharts` instance is created only when the component is rendered in a browser environment, avoiding the `ReferenceError`. The `afterNextRender()` hook guarantees your code runs after the initial render on the server and before the client-side rendering.

