The issue arises from how Angular tracks changes in the `@for` loop using the `$index` as the tracking expression. When you click on a button, it triggers a change in multiple items due to the shared index value. To fix this, use unique identifiers for each item instead of relying on the index.

Change your template to track by the `id` property:

```typescript
@for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
  <app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle(todo)" />
}
```

And ensure your `Todo` interface includes an `id` field:

```typescript
export interface Todo {
  id: number;
  done: boolean;
}
```

Update the `todos` array to include unique IDs:

```typescript
private todos = [
  { id: 1, done: false },
  { id: 2, done: false },
  { id: 3, done: false }
];
```

This change ensures that each item is uniquely tracked, preventing unintended state changes when interacting with the buttons.

