The confusion arises from understanding the roles of server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side routing in your Angular application. When you build an Angular app with SSR enabled, the initial HTML content is generated on the server. However, this does not mean that all components are pre-rendered; instead, it means the server sends a fully rendered HTML page to the browser.

The key point is that after receiving the initial HTML from the server, the client-side JavaScript takes over. The Angular router in your application will handle navigation and rendering of components dynamically on the client side. This explains why you see components in the `browser` folder post-build; they are intended for client-side execution.

To ensure SSR works correctly:
1. Configure your server to serve `index.html` when a requested file is not found.
2. Ensure your Angular app uses the `BrowserModule` on the client side and `ServerModule` with `RouterModule.forRoot()` for server-side routing.

Here’s a brief example of setting up an Express server:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const { join } = require('path');
const { enableProdMode } = require('@angular/core');

enableProdMode();

const app = express();
app.use(express.static(join(__dirname, 'browser')));

// Serve index.html for all other routes
app.get('*', (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile(join(__dirname, 'browser', 'index.html'));
});

app.listen(4000, () => console.log('Server running on port 4000'));
```

This setup ensures that the server serves `index.html`, allowing client-side routing to take over.

