To achieve lazy loading for each iframe in your Angular application, you can use a combination of `@for` and `@defer`. The key is to ensure that the components or elements inside the loop are deferred so they load only when necessary. Here’s how you can do it:

```angular-html
@for (link of youtubeLinks; track link.id) {
  @defer {
    <iframe [src]="link.url" frameborder="0"></iframe>
  }
}
```

In this code, each `iframe` is wrapped in a `@defer` block. This ensures that the iframe and its associated components are loaded only when needed, improving initial load times.

Additionally, to avoid layout shifts or flickering, you might want to add a loading placeholder inside the `@defer` block:

```angular-html
@for (link of youtubeLinks; track link.id) {
  @defer {
    <div *ngIf="!link.loaded" class="loading">Loading...</div>
    <iframe [src]="link.url" frameborder="0"></iframe>
  }
}
```

Here, a loading placeholder is shown until the iframe is fully loaded. This approach helps in maintaining a smooth user experience by preventing sudden changes in layout.

Remember to mark the `loaded` property as true once the iframe has finished loading to remove the placeholder and display the content.

