The behavior you're observing stems from how Angular handles references and copies in template-driven vs. structural directive rendering contexts.

When using `*ngFor`, Angular creates a new instance of the child component for each iteration, passing a reference to the current item. Any mutations directly on this reference will affect the original object because it's passed by reference.

However, when you use the `@for` block (part of Angular 13+ template syntax), it can create a copy of the input value if the type is complex (like your `ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem`). This means that even though the child component receives an instance of `ProblemGroup`, any direct mutation inside the child does not affect the original object because it's working with a shallow copy.

To ensure you're always working with references, consider using a getter in your parent component to return the correct type:

```typescript
// Parent Component
get activeProblem(): ProblemGroup | NarrativeProblem {
  if (this.activeProblem instanceof ProblemGroup) {
    return this.activeProblem as ProblemGroup;
  } else {
    return this.activeProblem;
  }
}
```

Then, pass `activeProblem` directly to the child component using `*ngFor`, ensuring that any mutations in the child reflect changes in the parent's original object.

