In Laravel 11, the `kernel.php` file might not be present by default as middleware registration can now occur in other service providers. To resolve the "Target class [permission] does not exist" error with Spatie Permission package, you need to register the necessary middleware in your `AppServiceProvider`.

Open or create the `app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php` file and add the following code inside the `boot` method:

```php
use Spatie\Permission\Middlewares\RoleMiddleware;
use Spatie\Permission\Middlewares\PermissionMiddleware;
use Spatie\Permission\Middlewares\RoleOrPermissionMiddleware;

public function boot()
{
    if ($this->app->routesAreCached()) {
        return;
    }

    \Route::middleware([
        'role' => RoleMiddleware::class,
        'permission' => PermissionMiddleware::class,
        'role_or_permission' => RoleOrPermissionMiddleware::class,
    ])->group(function () {
        // Define your routes here
    });
}
```

This registration ensures that the middleware is properly set up for handling permissions in your application. Make sure you have installed and configured Spatie Permission according to its documentation.

