Yes, there is already value in using signals even with the current zone-based change detection strategy. Signals can improve performance by reducing unnecessary change detections, especially in large component trees. They allow you to mark components for check more precisely and avoid `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenChecked` errors. For instance:

```typescript
const count = signal(0);

// Incrementing count will trigger change detection only when needed.
count.set(count() + 1);
```

While signals are not yet fully integrated into the zone-based strategy, they can still provide benefits in managing state and dependencies. As Angular continues to evolve, signals may gain more performance advantages, particularly with deeper integration in future releases.

