When you disable zone.js and update a signal directly with `update()`, Angular doesn't automatically trigger change detection because it relies on notifications from core APIs to run change detection, such as updates to signals read in templates or bound listeners. In your case, the template reads `{{ title() }}` but does not bind to changes in the signal.

To ensure the view updates, you need to manually mark the component for check after updating the signal:

```typescript
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
 selector: 'app-root',
 template: `
 {{ title() }} 
 Change 
 `,
})
export class AppComponent {
 title = signal('Hello');

 click(): void {
 this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
 // Manually trigger change detection
 const cdRef = (this as any).ngZone;
 if (cdRef && cdRef.markForCheck) {
   cdRef.markForCheck();
 }
 }
}
```

Alternatively, consider using a signal directly in the template with two-way binding or ensuring your component is properly marked for check.

