To retrieve the required parameter in your controller method, you don't necessarily need to use `Request $request`. The parameter from the route will be automatically passed as a method argument if you define your route correctly and bind the model or type-hint the parameter directly. For example:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\UserController;
use App\Models\User;

// Route definition...
Route::get('/users/{user}', [UserController::class, 'show']);

// Controller method definition...
public function show(User $user)
{
    return view('user.profile', ['user' => $user]);
}
```

In this case, the `User` model instance corresponding to the `{user}` parameter will be automatically injected into your controller's `show` method.

If you prefer not to use route-model binding and want to manually retrieve the parameter using the `Request` object, you can do so like this:

```php
public function some_method(Request $request)
{
    // Get the 'param' value from the request URI.
    $paramValue = $request->route('param');

    // Or directly access it as an argument in a closure route definition:
    return $request->route('param');  // Inside Route::get('/route/{param}', function (Request $request, string $param) { ... });
}
```

This approach is useful when you need more control over how the parameter is handled or if you're not using model binding.

