The issue arises because `$homeInfo` might be `null`, and you're trying to access properties on a null object. When using `first()`, if no record matches the query, it returns `null`. You should check if `$homeInfo` is not null before accessing its properties.

Here's how you can modify your Blade template:

```blade
@php
    $heroImage = $homeInfo ? $homeInfo->where('pag_name', 'hero_image')->first() : null;
@endphp

@if ($heroImage)
    <img src="{{ asset($heroImage->pag_value) }}" alt="Hero Image">
@endif
```

This ensures that you only attempt to access properties of `$heroImage` if it is not `null`. Similarly, apply this check for other pag_names.

Alternatively, in your repository method, consider returning a collection or an array instead of a single model. This way, you can handle multiple records more gracefully:

```php
public function getPageByType(string $pageType)
{
    return Page::where('pag_type', $pageType)->get();
}
```

Then, in Blade:

```blade
@foreach ($homeInfo as $info)
    @if ($info->pag_name == 'hero_image')
        <img src="{{ asset($info->pag_value) }}" alt="Hero Image">
    @endif
@endforeach
```

This approach ensures you handle multiple records and avoid null pointer exceptions.

