The issue you're experiencing with the session ID changing after each request in your feature tests is likely due to Laravel's default behavior of regenerating the session ID during authentication, which occurs by default in test environments. This can be mitigated by explicitly disabling session regeneration or blocking sessions for testing purposes.

To prevent session regeneration in your tests, you can use the `block` method on your route definitions:

```php
use Tests\TestCase;

class ExampleTest extends TestCase
{
    public function setUp(): void
    {
        parent::setUp();
        $this->withoutMiddleware(\Illuminate\Session\Middleware\StartSession::class);
    }

    public function test_interacting_with_cookies(): void
    {
        $response = $this->withCookie('color', 'blue')->get('/');

        // Test logic here...
    }
}
```

Alternatively, you can block sessions globally in your `app/Http/Middleware/StartSession.php`:

```php
public function handle($request, Closure $next)
{
    if (env('APP_ENV') !== 'testing') {
        return $next($request)->withSessionState();
    }

    return $next($request);
}
```

This ensures that sessions are not blocked in the testing environment, preventing session regeneration.

