In Angular 17 with standalone components, you can still nest one component inside another similar to how you did in previous versions. However, since standalone components don't require an `NgModule`, the approach slightly changes. Here’s a simple example:

First, create your nested components as standalone components:
```typescript
// banner.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-banner',
  template: '<h1>Welcome Banner</h1>'
})
export class BannerComponent {}
```

Then, in the parent component where you want to use `BannerComponent`, declare it as a dependency:
```typescript
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { BannerComponent } from './banner/banner.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <div>
      <app-banner></app-banner>
    </div>`
})
export class AppComponent {}
```

In the `template` of your parent component (`AppComponent`), you can now use `<app-banner>` as a regular HTML tag. This works because both components are standalone and properly declared.

Remember, when using standalone components, ensure all dependencies (like other components) are imported where they are used. This approach simplifies dependency management but requires careful attention to avoid circular imports or missing declarations.

