In Laravel, you can specify the controller class using either `use` statement or `->namespace()` in your route definition. Both methods are correct but serve slightly different purposes and contexts.

To directly use the controller without specifying its full path every time, include it with a `use` statement at the top of your routes file:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PhotoController;

Route::prefix('admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

Alternatively, if you prefer to manage namespaces within route groups for cleaner code, use `->namespace()`:

```php
Route::prefix('admin')->namespace('App\Http\Controllers\Admin')->middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);
});
```

Both methods are valid. The first approach is more explicit and can be useful in larger projects for clarity, while the second method keeps your route definitions cleaner by managing namespaces within the group.

In your case, since you're already using `->namespace()`, ensure that the class name in `Route::resource('photos', PhotoController::class);` matches exactly with the fully qualified class name. If it still shows "Class 'PhotoController' does not exist," double-check the namespace and class name spelling and existence.

