To refactor your `ngFor` loop for Angular v17 syntax and use the `trackBy` function correctly without adding an extra `ng-container`, you can directly place the template inside the `@for` directive like this:

```angular-html
@for (dt of totals; track $index) {
  <div [ngClass]="{'highlight': dt > 10}">{{ dt }}</div>
}
```

This approach leverages Angular's new `@for` syntax, which is equivalent to the older `*ngFor`. The `trackBy` function helps Angular efficiently manage changes in the list by providing a unique identifier for each item. In this case, `$index` serves as an identifier.

Remember that with `@for`, the element itself and its children are repeated, so you can directly include attributes like `[ngClass]` within the loop without needing an extra `ng-container`. This keeps your template clean and efficient.

