The issue you're encountering with contextual information not being merged correctly when specifying a logging channel in Laravel v10 might be due to how global context or per-channel context is handled. By default, Laravel applies global context to all log entries unless overridden by specific context for the channel.

To ensure that your custom context is applied to logs written through a specific channel, you should define this context globally or within the `bootstrap/app.php` file:

```php
$app->withExceptions(function (Exception $exception) {
    return function () use ($exception) {
        // Define global context here if needed.
        Log::useContext(fn () => [
            'user' => auth()->user()->id,
            'id' => $thing->id,
        ]);
    };
});
```

Alternatively, you can directly set the context for a specific channel when building it:

```php
$channel = Log::build([
    'driver' => 'single',
    'path' => storage_path('logs/mychannel.log'),
    'context' => [
        'user' => auth()->user()->id,
        'id' => $thing->id,
    ],
]);

Log::stack(['slack', $channel])->info('Something happened!');
```

This ensures that the context is applied to logs written through `mychannel`.

