To return a custom 404 view along with the HTTP 404 status code from your middleware, you should use Laravel's `abort` function to generate an HTTP exception. Here’s how you can modify your `handle` method:

```php
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
    if ($request->user() === null || $request->user()->role !== 'admin') {
        abort(404); // This will render the 404 view and set the HTTP status code to 404.
    }

    return $next($request);
}
```

By using `abort(404)`, Laravel will automatically look for a custom 404 view in your `resources/views/errors` directory (e.g., `errors/404.blade.php`). If no custom view is defined, it will render the default 404 page.

Ensure you have a proper 404 view set up or Laravel will use its default error handling. This approach ensures that when the conditions are not met, users see a meaningful 404 error page rather than a plain text response.

