To pass variables from your custom template to the vendor template in Laravel notifications, you can use the `view` method instead of `markdown`. This allows you to directly specify a view and its data, which will be available throughout that view hierarchy. Here’s how you can do it:

```php
public function toMail($notifiable): MailMessage
{
    return (new MailMessage)
                ->view('emails.reminder', ['unsubscribe_url' => 'custom_url_here']);
}
```

In your `emails.reminder` template, you can then use the variable like this:

```blade
{{-- Body --}}
{{ $slot }}

{{-- Subcopy --}}
@isset($subcopy)
    {{ $subcopy }}
@endisset

{{-- Footer --}}
Unsubscribe here: <a href="{{ $unsubscribe_url }}">Unsubscribe</a>

© {{ date('Y') }} {{ config('app.name') }}. @lang('All rights reserved.')
```

This approach ensures that the variable `unsubscribe_url` is available in your custom template and can be passed down to any nested components or layouts, including the vendor templates.

If you need to customize specific parts of the vendor template, consider extending it with your own layout file where you define the necessary variables. For example:

```blade
@extends('vendor.mail.html.message')

@section('content')
    {{ $slot }}

    <p>Unsubscribe here: <a href="{{ $unsubscribe_url }}">Unsubscribe</a></p>
@endsection
```

This way, you can maintain control over specific sections while leveraging the pre-built components.

