The issue you're encountering likely stems from how services and their dependencies are being managed in standalone components within Angular 17. When using `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })` for your service, ensure that the service is properly provided at the root level or a higher level in the injector hierarchy.

Since your component is standalone, you need to provide the `HttpClientModule` and any required services directly within the component's metadata:

```typescript
@Component({
  standalone: true,
  imports: [HttpClientModule], // Import HttpClientModule here
  template: `<p>Component Content</p>`
})
export class YourComponent {
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {} // Inject HttpClient here
}
```

Alternatively, if you want to keep your service as a separate entity, ensure it is provided at the root level or in an `@NgModule`:

```typescript
@NgModule({
  providers: [YourService] // Provide your service here
})
export class AppModule {}
```

For standalone services and components, consider using the `providedIn: 'root'` token directly within the service decorator to ensure it is available throughout the application.

If you need a more isolated setup without affecting other parts of the app, use `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'current'} )` in your service:

```typescript
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'current' })
export class YourService {
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
}
```

This ensures the service is only provided within the current injector context.

