The issue arises from how Angular handles tracking in `@for` loops and component reusability. When you use the `SliderComponent` directly within your template, each instance of the component operates independently because they are distinct components with their own state. However, when you track by `$index` in the `@for` loop, all instances share the same tracking expression, leading to unintended state changes.

In the provided code:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    @for (todo of undoneTodos; track $index) {
      <app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle($event, todo)" />
    }
  `,
  imports: [SliderComponent]
})
export class App {
  // ...
  toggle(todo: Todo, active: boolean) {
    todo.done = active;
    this.buildTodos();
  }
}
```

Here, tracking by `$index` means all `SliderComponent` instances share the same tracking expression. Clicking on one slider changes the state of all sliders because they are bound to the same index-based tracking.

To fix this, use a unique identifier for each todo item in the `@for` loop:

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    @for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
      <app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle($event, todo)" />
    }
  `,
  imports: [SliderComponent]
})
export class App {
  // ...
  toggle(active: boolean, todo: Todo) {
    todo.done = active;
    this.buildTodos();
  }
}
```

This ensures each `SliderComponent` instance has its own unique tracking expression, preventing unintended state changes.

