Yes, it's possible to achieve both functionalities in your `HistoryController`. The issue with pagination not working might be due to how you're handling the data passed to the view. Here’s a refined approach:

```php
public function index()
{
    $doneProjects = Project::where('status', 'Done')->orderBy('id', 'desc')->paginate(5);
    $softDeletedProjects = Project::onlyTrashed()->orderBy('id', 'desc')->get();

    // Merge the two collections, ensuring pagination works correctly for done projects
    $projects = $doneProjects->merge($softDeletedProjects);

    return view('history', compact('projects'));
}
```

In this solution:
- `Project::where('status', 'Done')` fetches projects with a status of "Done".
- `Project::onlyTrashed()` retrieves soft-deleted projects.
- The `paginate(5)` method is called on the done projects to ensure pagination works as expected.
- Both collections are merged into one, ensuring that both types of data are displayed.

Make sure your view (`history.blade.php`) can handle this merged collection. If you need to differentiate between done and soft-deleted projects in the view, you can use conditional checks on each project instance:

```blade
@foreach ($projects as $project)
    @if ($project->trashed())
        <p>Soft Deleted Project: {{ $project->name }}</p>
    @else
        <p>Done Project: {{ $project->name }}</p>
    @endif
@endforeach
```

This approach ensures that both types of projects are displayed correctly and pagination works as expected.

