To dynamically populate a dropdown in the registration form using data from a database table (e.g., "genders"), you need to pass the `$genders` collection to your Blade view. Here’s how:

1. **Retrieve Data**: In `app/Providers/FortifyServiceProvider.php`, use the `boot` method to load the genders into the session or directly pass them to the view.

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Gate;
use Laravel\Fortify\Features;

public function boot()
{
    parent::boot();

    // Pass genders data to the Fortify registration view
    Fortify::registerView(function () {
        return view('auth.register', ['genders' => Genders::all()]);
    });
}
```

2. **Display in View**: In your `resources/views/auth/register.blade.php`, use a Blade loop to display the genders.

```blade
<div>
    <x-label for="gender" :value="__('Select Gender')" />

    <select name="gender_id" id="gender">
        @foreach ($genders as $gender)
            <option value="{{ $gender->id }}">{{ $gender->name }}</option>
        @endforeach
    </select>

    <x-input-error for="gender" />
</div>
```

This approach ensures that the genders are dynamically fetched from your database and passed to the Blade view, making it easy to extend this method for other data like countries.

