The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular interprets and processes the `@for` directive in combination with conditional blocks like `@if`. The error message indicates that Angular expects a block of content within your `@if` condition, but it's not finding one. 

To achieve your goal, you can structure your template as follows:

```angular-html
@for (order of licenseOverview.orders; track order.id) {
  @if ($first) {
    <tr>
      <th>Order</th>
      <th>Expected On</th>
      <th>Status</th>
    </tr>
  }
  {{ order.reference }}
  <!-- Add other fields here -->

  @if ($last) {
    <!-- Add table footer if needed -->
  }
}
@empty { No data for you! }
```

This approach ensures that the `@if` conditions are properly structured within their respective blocks. The `$first` and `$last` variables provided by Angular can be used to conditionally render the header and footer of your table.

Remember, when using `@for`, it's crucial to keep the structure clean and ensure all conditional logic is correctly placed within its designated block.

