In your scenario, you can indeed avoid creating an observable from the signal just for the side effect of enabling or disabling a FormControl. The `toObservable` utility function can be used to directly trigger side effects based on the signal's changes without needing to subscribe manually. Here’s how you can do it:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { toObservable } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

@Component({...})
export class YourComponent {
  isItEditor = inject(IsItEditorService).isItEditor;
  isItEditor$ = toObservable(this.isItEditor);

  constructor(private formGroup: FormGroup, private destroyRef: DestroyRef) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.isItEditor$.pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)).subscribe(x => {
      const funded = this.formGroup.controls.funded;
      if (x) funded.enable();
      else funded.disable();
    });
  }
}
```

By using `toObservable`, you can leverage the Observable's subscription mechanism to handle side effects directly, making your code cleaner and more aligned with Angular’s reactive programming model.

