Your approach is on the right track but can be improved for maintainability and alignment with Laravel conventions. Instead of including migration files directly, you can use Laravel's built-in mechanisms to handle migrations more elegantly. Here’s a cleaner way:

1. **Define Migrations in an Array**: Store your migration classes in an array.
2. **Use Artisan Commands**: Leverage Artisan commands for running specific migrations.

First, define the migrations in `app/Console/Commands/RefreshMyTables.php`:

```php
namespace App\Console\Commands;

use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;
use Illuminate\Console\Command;

class RefreshMyTables extends Command
{
    protected $signature = 'refresh-my-tables';

    protected $description = 'Refresh specific migrations and seeders';

    public function handle()
    {
        // Define the migration classes
        $migrations = [
            \App\Migrations\CreateMyTableNumbersTable::class,
            \App\Migrations\CreateMyTableFilesTable::class,
            \App\Migrations\CreateMyTableOrdersTable::class,
            \App\Migrations\CreateMyTableTable::class,
        ];

        // Reverse the migrations
        foreach ($migrations as $migration) {
            DB::statement('SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS=0');
            (new $migration)->down();
            DB::statement('SET FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS=1');
        }

        // Run the migrations in order
        foreach ($migrations as $migration) {
            (new $migration)->up();

            // Optionally, run seeders after each migration if needed
            // (new \App\Seeders\MyTableSeeder())->run();
        }
    }
}
```

This approach avoids including long filenames and leverages Laravel's PSR-4 autoloading. You can now run `php artisan refresh-my-tables` to manage your specific migrations efficiently.

For seeders, you might want to consider running them separately or within a transaction for better control over the database state during testing.

