To link PDF files stored in `storage/app/public/pdf` from your view, you should use Laravel's `asset()` helper function correctly. The issue with your current approach lies in the incorrect usage of double curly braces and quotes. Here’s how you can fix it:

```blade
@foreach($report_archives as $report_archive)
    {{$report_archive->date}} 
    {{$report_archive->reportType}} 
    {{$report_archive->customerID}} 
    {{$report_archive->productID}} 
    {{asset('storage/reportLink.pdf')}}}}
@endforeach
```

Replace `reportLink.pdf` with the actual filename stored in your database. If you want to dynamically generate the URL based on the filename from `$report_archive`, modify it as follows:

```blade
@foreach($report_archives as $report_archive)
    {{$report_archive->date}} 
    {{$report_archive->reportType}} 
    {{$report_archive->customerID}} 
    {{$report_archive->productID}} 
    {{asset('storage/' . $report_archive->reportLink)}}}}
@endforeach
```

Ensure that the `reportLink` field in your database contains the correct filename (e.g., `Report.pdf`). Also, make sure you have run the `php artisan storage:link` command to create a symbolic link from `public/storage` to `storage/app/public`.

This setup will generate URLs like `/storage/Report.pdf`, which should correctly point to your PDF files stored in the public directory.

