The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular handles signals and change detection in `OnPush` components. When you disable zone.js with `{ ngZone: 'noop' }`, Angular's default change detection strategy, which relies on the zone for detecting changes, is bypassed. However, signals still need to be manually marked as dirty for the view to update.

In your example, the `AppComponent` uses a signal but doesn't explicitly mark itself or any dependent components for checking. To ensure the view updates when you call `title.update()`, you should manually trigger change detection after updating the signal:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    {{ title() }} 
    Change 
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually trigger change detection
    this.ngZone.markForCheck();
  }

  constructor(private ngZone: NgZone) {}
}
```

By calling `this.ngZone.markForCheck()`, you instruct Angular to run change detection and update the view accordingly. This approach ensures that even without zone.js, changes to signals are properly reflected in the UI.

