To retrieve the required parameter in your controller method, you don't need to use `Request $request` explicitly if the route has already type-hinted the model or parameter. However, if you want to access the raw value of a route parameter (e.g., 'param' from '/route/{param}'), you can do so by defining the parameter in your controller method signature.

For example:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\UserController;

// Route definition...
Route::get('/route/{param}', [UserController::class, 'some_method']);

// Controller method definition...
public function some_method(string $param)
{
    // Use $param directly here.
    return "The parameter value is: {$param}";
}
```

In this case, Laravel automatically injects the value of `{param}` into your controller method. This approach leverages implicit binding and route model binding principles described in the documentation.

If you need to use `Request` for additional logic or when type-hinting isn't sufficient, you can still do so:

```php
public function some_method(Request $request, string $param)
{
    // Use both $param and $request here.
    return "The parameter value is: {$param} - Additional request data: {$request->input('someKey')}";
}
```

This way, you can access the route parameter directly while also having full access to the `Request` object for more complex operations.

