Yes, you can load a standalone component in Angular 16 as a dialog without using routing or preloading. Since your component is standalone, you don't need to declare it in any module. Here’s how you can do it:

First, ensure your standalone component is properly defined and marked with `@Component({standalone: true})`.

To open this component as a dialog, you can use the Angular Material Dialog service or a third-party library like PrimeNG's Dialog.

Here’s an example using Angular Material:

1. Import necessary modules:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { MatDialog } from '@angular/material/dialog';
```

2. Inject `MatDialog` in your component where you want to open the dialog.
3. Open the dialog when needed, passing the standalone component as a parameter.

Example:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-your-component',
  template: `<button (click)="openDialog()">Open Dialog</button>`
})
export class YourComponent {
  constructor(private dialog: MatDialog) {}

  openDialog() {
    this.dialog.open(YourStandaloneComponent);
  }
}
```

Ensure `YourStandaloneComponent` is correctly defined as a standalone component. This approach leverages Angular's built-in capabilities to dynamically load and display components without the need for routing or preloading.

For more detailed guidance, refer to the official Angular documentation on [standalone components](https://angular.io/guide/standalone-components) and [Angular Material Dialog](https://material.angular.io/components/dialog/overview).

