To mock an external class with constructor parameters in Laravel without creating a factory, you can use the `resolve` method from the service container directly within your test case. This approach allows you to pass the required constructor arguments during the resolution process. Here's how you can do it:

```php
use App\Service;
use Mockery\MockInterface;

// Inside your test function
$mock = $this->mock(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, function (MockInterface $mock) {
    $mock->shouldReceive('make')->once()->andReturn($mock);
    $mock->shouldReceive('verify')->once()->andReturn(true); // Adjust the return value as needed
});

// Resolve and pass constructor arguments directly
$recaptcha = resolve(\ReCaptcha\ReCaptcha::class, ['secret' => config('services.recaptcha.secret')]);

// Now you can use $recaptcha in your test logic
```

This method leverages Laravel's service container to resolve the class with its required parameters and mock its methods directly within the test. This approach reduces unnecessary code and keeps your tests clean and focused.

