Your approach of using `ChangeDetectorRef` to manually trigger change detection is necessary for OnPush components because Angular won't automatically detect changes in certain scenarios. Here’s a concise solution:

1. Ensure your component uses `OnPush` change detection:
   ```typescript
   @Component({
     selector: 'app-login',
     templateUrl: './login.component.html',
     styleUrls: ['./login.component.css'],
     changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
   })
   export class LoginComponent {
     isSubmitting = false;
     // ...
   }
   ```

2. Use `ChangeDetectorRef` to manually trigger change detection after state changes:
   ```typescript
   constructor(private ref: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

   login() {
     this.isSubmitting = true;
     this.auth.login(this.username.value, this.password.value, this.rememberMe.value)
       .pipe(filter(authenticated => authenticated))
       .subscribe({
         next: () => {
           this.isSubmitting = false;
           this.ref.markForCheck(); // Manually trigger change detection
         },
       });
   }
   ```

3. Ensure your template correctly binds to `isSubmitting`:
   ```html
   <button [disabled]="isSubmitting" mat-raised-button>
     {{'login' | translate}}
     <mat-spinner *ngIf="isSubmitting"></mat-spinner>
   </button>
   ```

By using `ChangeDetectorRef`, you ensure Angular re-evaluates the template, updating the button state as expected.

