To address the issue of showing a loading status during lazy-loaded route transitions, you can implement a global loading indicator that displays while the authorized routes are being loaded after login. Here’s how:

1. **Create a Loading Component**: First, create a simple `LoadingComponent` to display your loading state.

2. **Modify Routing Configuration**: Update your routing configuration to include this component during the transition period.

Here's an example of how you can do it:

```typescript
// app.routes.ts
export const appRoutes: Routes = [
  { path: '', redirectTo: '/dashboard', pathMatch: 'full' },
  {
    path: 'login',
    component: LoginComponent,
    title: 'Login',
  },
  {
    path: '',
    canActivateChild: [AuthGuard],
    loadChildren: () => import(`./app-authorized.routes`).then((r) => r.appAuthorizedRoutes),
    // Add a wildcard route to catch the loading state
    children: [
      { path: '', component: LoadingComponent, title: 'Loading' },
    ],
  },
  { path: '**', redirectTo: '/dashboard' },
];

// app-authorized.routes.ts
export const appAuthorizedRoutes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'dashboard',
    component: DashboardComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Dashboard',
  },
  {
    path: 'settings',
    component: SettingsComponent,
    canActivate: [AuthGuard],
    title: 'Settings',
  },
  // etc...
];
```

In this setup, when the user logs in and is redirected to the authorized routes, a `LoadingComponent` will be displayed until the lazy-loaded modules are fully loaded. This ensures that your UI looks consistent during the transition period.

For more detailed steps on implementing loading indicators, refer to the [Lazy Loading](guide/ngmodules/lazy-loading) guide provided in the documentation excerpts.

