The issue you're encountering stems from how contextual information is managed in different logging channels within Laravel. When using `Log::channel('mychannel')->info()`, the context set by `Log::withContext()` or `Log::shareContext()` does not automatically apply to that specific channel.

To ensure your custom context is applied when logging to a specific channel, you should use `Log::withChannelContext('mychannel')` before writing the log entry. Here’s how you can modify your code:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;
use Illuminate\Support\Str;

// Set context for 'mychannel'
Log::withChannelContext('mychannel', [
    'request-id' => (string) Str::uuid()
]);

// Log to 'mychannel' with contextual information
Log::channel('mychannel')->info(
    'User {user} created Thing {id}',
    [
        'user' => auth()->user()->id,
        'id' => $thing->id
    ]
);
```

This approach ensures that the context is correctly applied to logs written through `Log::channel('mychannel')`.

