The issue arises from how Angular handles tracking in the `@for` directive. When you use `track $index`, Angular re-renders all items in the list whenever any item's state changes, leading to unintended side effects. This is because the entire DOM structure is recreated for each iteration.

To fix this, use a unique identifier instead of `$index`. For example:

```typescript
@for (todo of undoneTodos; track todo.id) {
  <app-slider [active]="todo.done" (activeChange)="toggle(todo)"></app-slider>
}
```

This ensures that only the relevant items are updated when their state changes, preventing the unintended state change in other items.

By using `todo.id` for tracking, Angular can efficiently update only the necessary parts of the DOM, maintaining correct behavior.

