The error you're encountering (`document is not defined`) suggests that Bootstrap's JavaScript is trying to access the DOM before it has been fully initialized in a standalone component environment. This can happen because Angular's standalone components do not automatically bootstrap with the same lifecycle as traditional NgModule-based components.

To resolve this issue, ensure that your custom elements are properly bootstrapped and interact with the DOM after it is ready. Here’s how you can address it:

1. **Ensure `@angular/elements` is installed**:
   ```shell
   npm install @angular/elements --save
   ```

2. **Convert your root component to standalone mode**:
   Update your root component and module as follows:
   ```typescript
   // app.component.ts
   import { Component, ViewEncapsulation } from '@angular/core';

   @Component({
     selector: 'app-root',
     template: `<ng-container *ngIf="ready; else loading">Hello World</ng-container>
                <ng-template #loading>Initializing...</ng-template>`,
     encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.None,
     standalone: true
   })
   export class AppComponent {
     ready = false;

     ngAfterViewInit() {
       this.ready = true;
     }
   }

   // app.module.ts (if you have one)
   import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
   import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';

   @NgModule({
     imports: [BrowserModule],
     declarations: [AppComponent],
     bootstrap: [AppComponent]
   })
   export class AppModule {}
   ```

3. **Bootstrap your application with `bootstrapApplication`**:
   ```typescript
   // main.ts
   import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
   import { AppComponent } from './app.component';

   bootstrapApplication(AppComponent);
   ```

By ensuring that your components are properly initialized and interact with the DOM after it is ready, you should be able to resolve the `document is not defined` error.

