In Laravel 11, you can register custom middleware in your `AppServiceProvider`. Specifically, you should use the `register` method to bind your middleware as a singleton with the service container. This ensures that the same instance of the middleware is used for both handling requests and terminating them.

Here’s how you can do it:

```php
use App\Http\Middleware\YourCustomMiddleware;

public function register(): void
{
    $this->app->singleton(YourCustomMiddleware::class);
}
```

After registering, you can apply this middleware to routes or controllers as needed. For example, to apply your custom middleware to a route:

```php
Route::get('/your-route', [YourController::class, 'yourMethod'])->middleware('your_custom_middleware');
```

If you need to alias the middleware for convenience (especially useful for long class names), you can do so in the `withMiddleware` method within your `AppServiceProvider`'s `boot` method:

```php
use App\Http\Middleware\YourCustomMiddleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
    $middleware->alias([
        'your_custom_middleware' => YourCustomMiddleware::class,
    ]);
})
```

This setup ensures that your custom middleware is properly registered and can be easily managed within your Laravel 11 application.

