The error "localStorage is not defined" occurs because `localStorage` is a browser API and might not be available in non-browser environments like Node.js during testing or server-side rendering (SSR). To resolve this issue, you can use the `@ngx-pwa/local-storage` package to provide a polyfill for `localStorage`.

1. Install the package:
   ```sh
   npm install @ngx-pwa/local-storage --save
   ```

2. Import and configure it in your Angular application's main module (e.g., `app.module.ts`):
   ```typescript
   import { NgxPwaLocalStorageModule } from '@ngx-pwa/local-storage';

   @NgModule({
     imports: [
       // other imports...
       NgxPwaLocalStorageModule.forRoot()
     ],
     // ...
   })
   export class AppModule {}
   ```

3. Update your `AuthService` to use the polyfilled `localStorage`:
   ```typescript
   import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
   import { Router } from '@angular/router';
   import { BehaviorSubject, Observable } from 'rxjs';

   @Injectable({
     providedIn: 'root',
   })
   export class AuthService {
     public isAuth = new BehaviorSubject(false);

     constructor(private router: Router) {
       this.autoSignIn();
     }

     autoSignIn() {
       if (this.localStorage.getItem('email')) { // Use getItem method
         this.isAuth.next(true);
         this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
       }
     }

     signIn(email: string) {
       this.localStorage.setItem('email', email); // Use setItem method
       this.isAuth.next(true);
       this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
     }

     signOut() {
       this.localStorage.removeItem('email'); // Use removeItem method
       this.isAuth.next(false);
       this.router.navigate(['/auth']);
     }
   }
   ```

By using the `@ngx-pwa/local-storage` package, you ensure that your application works correctly both in browser and non-browser environments.

