To achieve conditional rendering with `@if` and `@else` in Angular 17 without combining statements or using logical OR (`||`) for type safety, you can structure your template as follows:

```angular-ts
template: `
  @if (movies && movies.length > 0) {
    Favorites 
    Recent {{movies[movies.length-1].title}} - {{movies[movies.length-1].rating}}
    Total {{movies.length}}
  } @else {
    Favorites 
    Recent NA
    Total 0
  }
`;
```

This approach uses `@if` to check if `movies` exists and has items. If the condition is true, it displays the detailed content; otherwise, it shows a fallback message. This avoids type errors by ensuring that only when `movies` is truthy and non-empty does the template attempt to access its properties.

Remember, in Angular 17, you can use native template syntax like `@if` and `@else` directly as shown above without needing to switch back to older directives or workarounds.

