By adding `?` to the property accesses, you ensure that the template will not throw an error if `posts`, `post.id`, or `post.title` is undefined. This approach aligns with the documentation excerpt suggesting to "add `null` or `undefined` to its type to indicate this" when dealing with nullable values.

Remember to enable full template type checking by adding `"fullTemplateTypeCheck": true` in your Angular compiler options if you haven't already done so, as described in phase 3 of the template type-checking process.

