The issue you're encountering arises from how Angular's template syntax handles `@if` and `@for`. The `@if` directive expects a single element to be rendered, but your attempt to conditionally render multiple elements within the same block is causing an error. 

To achieve your goal of showing either a table or a message when there's no data, you can structure your template as follows:

```html
@for(order of licenseOverview.orders; track order.id) {
  @if($first) {
    {{ 'Order' }} 
    {{ 'Expected On' }} 
    {{ 'Status' }} 
  }
  {{ order.reference }}
  {{ ... }}
}
@empty { No data for you! }
```

This approach ensures that the header is only rendered once, and it works correctly with `@for` and `@empty`. The `$first` variable from `@for` can be used to conditionally render the table headers. This way, Angular will handle rendering the table or the "No data" message appropriately based on the presence of data in `licenseOverview.orders`.

Remember, `@if` should wrap a single element or block that you want to conditionally include.

