In Laravel 11, middleware related to Spatie Permission package has been simplified and moved into the framework itself. You no longer need a separate `Kernel.php` file for adding these middlewares as they are automatically included in your application's bootstrapping process.

To resolve the "Target class [permission] does not exist" error when using the Spatie Permission package, you should remove the middleware registration from your `app/Http/Kernel.php` if it exists. Laravel 11 no longer requires these explicit registrations for basic functionality.

Instead, ensure you have correctly installed and configured the Spatie Permission package according to its documentation. Focus on setting up the necessary migrations and seeding roles and permissions as described in the package's README.

If you need custom middleware behavior, you can customize it via your application’s `bootstrap/app.php` file, as shown in the documentation:

```php
->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
    // Customize middleware here if needed.
})
```

This approach leverages Laravel 11's improved framework structure to handle package dependencies more efficiently.

