To achieve the desired output in Laravel using Eloquent, you can use the `sum` method provided by the query builder or collection methods. Here's how you can do it:

1. First, retrieve your data as a collection.
2. Use the `groupBy` and `sum` methods to aggregate the quantities.

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

$items = Item::all(); // Assuming 'Item' is your model

$result = $items->groupBy('barcode')
               ->map(function ($item) {
                   return [
                       'id' => $item->first()->id,
                       'barcode' => $item->first()->barcode,
                       'description' => $item->first()->description,
                       'qty' => $item->sum('quantity'),
                   ];
               });

// Alternatively, using query builder
$result = DB::table('items')
           ->select(
               DB::raw("MIN(id) as id"),
               'barcode',
               'description',
               DB::raw("SUM(quantity) as qty")
           )
           ->groupBy('barcode', 'description')
           ->get();
```

This code groups the items by `barcode` and then sums up the `quantity` for each group. The result is a collection of arrays with the desired structure.

If you prefer to use Eloquent collections directly, you can also do:

```php
$items = Item::all();

$result = $items->groupBy('barcode')
               ->map(function ($item) {
                   return [
                       'id' => $item->first()->id,
                       'barcode' => $item->first()->barcode,
                       'description' => $item->first()->description,
                       'qty' => $item->sum('quantity'),
                   ];
               });
```

This approach leverages the `groupBy` and `map` methods to transform your collection into the desired format.

