In your scenario, since you don't have an `app.module.ts` file but instead use `app.config.ts` and `app.config.server.ts`, you should place the `provideHttpClient()` call within the providers array of one of these configuration files. Given that both configurations are part of the same application setup, it's best to consolidate the HTTP client provider in a single location for clarity.

Since `app.config.ts` is used for the main browser environment and `app.config.server.ts` for server-side rendering, you can place `provideHttpClient()` in `app.config.ts`. This ensures that the HttpClient module is properly configured when your application runs on both the client and server sides. Here’s how you can do it:

```ts
// app.config.ts

import { ApplicationConfig, provideZoneChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
import { provideRouter, withComponentInputBinding } from '@angular/router';
import { routes } from './app.routes';
import { provideClientHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true }),
    provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()),
    provideClientHydration(),
    provideHttpClient() // Place this here
  ]
};
```

This approach ensures that `provideHttpClient()` is correctly configured for your application.

