To join data from multiple tables in Laravel resources and return them via API, you need to properly define relationships between your models first. In your case, you should define a relationship method in the `Beasiswa` model that joins with the `Mitra` model. Here's how you can do it:

1. Define the relationship in the `Beasiswa` model:
```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Beasiswa extends Model
{
    public function mitra()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Mitra::class, 'id_mitra', 'id');
    }
}
```

2. Modify your resource to use the relationship:
```php
namespace App\Http\Resources\V1;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\Resources\Json\JsonResource;

class BeasiswaResource extends JsonResource
{
    public function toArray(Request $request): array
    {
        return [
            'idBeasiswa' => $this->id,
            'idMitra' => $this->mitra ? $this->mitra->id : null,
            'deskripsi' => $this->deskripsi,
            'angkatanAwal' => $this->angkatan_awal,
            'angkatanAkhir' => $this->angkatan_akhir,
            'semMin' => $this->sem_min,
            'semMax' => $this->sem_max,
            'status' => $this->status
        ];
    }
}
```

3. Ensure your controller returns the resource correctly:
```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers\Api\V1;

use App\Models\Beasiswa;
use App\Http\Controllers\Controller;
use App\Http\Resources\V1\BeasiswaResource;

class BeasiswaController extends Controller
{
    public function index()
    {
        return new BeasiswaCollection(Beasiswa::with('mitra')->paginate());
    }
}
```

This setup ensures that the `id_mitra` is correctly linked to a `Mitra` model instance, and you can access its attributes through `$this->mitra`.

