In your scenario, you don't necessarily need to convert the signal to an observable just for side effects. The `toObservable` utility can be used to monitor changes in a signal and trigger actions based on those changes, similar to how you were using an observable before.

Here’s how you could implement it:

```ts
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

@Component(...)
export class YourComponent {
  isItEditor: Signal<boolean> = inject(SomeService).isItEditor;

  // Use toObservable to track changes in the signal and apply side effects
  isItEditor$ = toObservable(this.isItEditor);

  constructor(private readonly destroyRef: DestroyRef) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.isItEditor$.pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef)).subscribe(x => {
      const funded = this.formGroup.controls.funded;
      if (x) {
        funded.enable();
      } else {
        funded.disable();
      }
    });
  }
}
```

This approach leverages `toObservable` to monitor the signal and apply side effects directly, avoiding unnecessary complexity. Remember, `toObservable` creates an observable that tracks changes in the signal, making it easier to handle side effects without needing to manually manage subscriptions.

