The issue you're encountering stems from how signals and zone handling interact in Angular. When you disable zone.js using `ngZone: 'noop'` in your bootstrap configuration, change detection does not automatically run even if a signal updates. To ensure the view updates after calling `update`, you need to manually trigger change detection.

Here’s how you can modify your code:

1. Import and inject `ChangeDetectorRef` into your component.
2. Call `changeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` after updating the signal.

```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectorRef } from '@angular/core';
import { signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <div>{{ title() }}</div>
    <button (click)="click()">Change</button>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = signal('Hello');

  constructor(private changeDetectorRef: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

  click(): void {
    this.title.update((value) => value + "!!");
    // Manually trigger change detection
    this.changeDetectorRef.detectChanges();
  }
}
```

By manually invoking `changeDetectorRef.detectChanges()`, you ensure that Angular processes the updated signal and updates the view accordingly.

