The issue with your controller and view seems to stem from how you are assigning variables in the `compact` function. In your controller, `$plans = $this->plans = DailyTaskPlan::...` assigns the value twice; once to `$plans` and again to a non-existent property `$this->plans`. This second assignment is unnecessary and can be removed.

Here's the corrected controller code:

```php
public function dailyTaskPlan()
{
    $plans = DailyTaskPlan::select(
        'projects.project_name',
        'daily_task_plans.*',
        'tasks.heading',
        'users.id')
    ->leftJoin('projects', 'daily_task_plans.project_id', '=', 'projects.id')
    ->leftJoin('tasks', 'daily_task_plans.task_id', '=', 'tasks.id')
    ->join('users','daily_task_plans.user_id', '=','users.id')
    ->take(4)
    ->get();

    return view('dailytaskplan::dashboard', compact('plans'));
}
```

Regarding merging module routes with project routes, you can use route aliasing or group your routes in a way that prioritizes the main application routes. Here’s an example of how to do this:

In `routes/web.php` (or your main route file):

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\DalyTaskPlanController;

Route::group(['prefix' => 'tasks'], function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [DalyTaskPlanController::class, 'dailyTaskPlan'])->name('daily-task-plan');
});
```

This way, the `/tasks/dashboard` route will be handled by your `DalyTaskPlanController`, while other routes defined in the main application should not be overridden. Adjust the prefix and controller method as needed to fit your project structure.

For more complex scenarios, consider using middleware or custom routing logic to manage priority between different route groups.

