The issue you're encountering stems from how Angular's change detection works with arrays. When you use `houses.push(payload.new)` or filter the array, Angular doesn't detect these changes because it compares references rather than values. To trigger the effect, you need to create a new array reference.

Here’s how you can modify your code:

```typescript
case "INSERT":
  this.houses.update(houses => {
    const updatedHouses = [...houses, payload.new]; // Create a new array with spread operator
    return updatedHouses;
  });
break;

case "DELETE":
  this.houses.update(houses => houses.filter(house => house.id !== payload.old.id)); // This should work as expected
```

By using the spread operator (`...`), you create a new array reference, which Angular can detect. This will trigger your effect.

Alternatively, if you're updating an object within the array, consider creating a new object to force a change detection:

```typescript
case "UPDATE":
  this.houses.update(houses => {
    const updatedHouses = houses.map(house =>
      house.id === payload.id ? { ...house, ...payload.newValues } : house
    );
    return updatedHouses;
  });
```

This approach ensures that Angular recognizes the change and triggers your effect.

