The issue you're encountering stems from how services are provided in Angular. While using `inject` works for accessing services within components and directives, it doesn't work for guards or other providers that need to access services during the bootstrapping phase of your application.

To resolve this, ensure your `UserService` is properly provided at the root level. Update your `app.module.ts` (or the module where you define your routing) as follows:

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';
import { UserService } from './users/user.service';

const routes: Routes = [
  // Your routes here
];

@NgModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes)],
  exports: [RouterModule],
  providers: [UserService] // Ensure UserService is provided at the root level
})
export class AppRoutingModule {}
```

Alternatively, if you want to provide `UserService` in a more flexible manner (e.g., per module), use:

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';
import { UserService } from './users/user.service';

@NgModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes)],
  exports: [RouterModule],
  providers: [UserService] // Provide in the specific module if needed
})
export class AppRoutingModule {}
```

By providing `UserService` at the root level, you ensure it is available during the bootstrapping process and can be injected into guards like your `authGuard`.

