The official way to refactor `*ngFor` to Angular v17 syntax while keeping your template clean involves leveraging the `@for` directive and understanding that it repeats both the element itself and its children. You can directly use the iteration variable within the `@for` block without needing an extra `ng-container`.

Here’s how you can refactor:

```html
@for (dt of totals; track $index) {
  <div [ngClass]="{'highlight': dt > 10}">
    {{ dt }}
  </div>
}
```

This approach works because the `@for` directive repeats the entire block, including any attributes and child elements. The iteration variable `dt` is available within the block, allowing you to use it directly with `[ngClass]`.

Remember, in Angular v17, `*ngFor` has been replaced by the more powerful `@for`, but the core concept remains: the entire template inside the directive is repeated for each item.

