To address the `ReferenceError: window is not defined` issue in your Angular 17 SSR application with ApexCharts, you can use the `afterNextRender()` lifecycle hook to ensure that ApexCharts initialization runs only on the client side. Here’s how you can modify your code:

```typescript
import { Component, ViewChild, afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';
import ApexCharts from 'apexcharts';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  templateUrl: './dashboard.component.html'
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  @ViewChild('chart') chart!: ElementRef;

  constructor() {}

  afterNextRenderCallback = () => {
    const element = this.chart.nativeElement;
    if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && element) { // Ensure we are in the browser
      new ApexCharts(element, {
        series: [
          { name: 'STOCK ABC', data: [/* your data */] },
        ],
        chart: { type: 'line' }, // Add other options as needed
      }).render();
    }
  };

  afterNextRender(this.afterNextRenderCallback);
}
```

This approach ensures that ApexCharts is only initialized when the application runs in a browser environment, avoiding the `ReferenceError`. The `afterNextRender()` hook guarantees this initialization happens on the client side.

