The issue you're encountering stems from how contextual information is handled in different logging channels within Laravel. When specifying a channel name with `Log::channel('mychannel')`, the contextual information set globally or via middleware might not be automatically applied to that specific channel unless explicitly shared.

To ensure your contextual information is included when using a specific log channel, you can use the `withContext` method on the desired channel:

```php
Log::channel('mychannel')->withContext([
    'user_id' => auth()->user()->id,
])->info(
    'User {user} created Thing {id}',
    ['id' => $thing->id]
);
```

Alternatively, if you want to share this context across all channels, use `Log::shareContext` in a middleware or service provider:

```php
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
    Log::shareContext([
        'user_id' => auth()->user()->id,
    ]);

    return $next($request);
}
```

This approach ensures that the context is applied to all log entries, regardless of which channel they are written to.

