To migrate your Angular templates from older syntax to the new one introduced in Angular 17, you can follow these steps:

1. **For `ngIf` (now `@if`)**:
   - Replace `*ngIf="condition"` with `@if (condition) { ... } @else { ... }`.
   - For example, your old template:
     ```html
     <div *ngIf="!dynamicWidth">
       <!-- content -->
     </div>
     ```
     Can be updated to the new syntax as follows:
     ```angular-ts
     @if (!dynamicWidth) {
       <!-- content -->
     }
     ```

2. **For `ngFor` (now `@for`)**:
   - Replace `*ngFor="let item of items"` with `@for (item of items; track item) { ... }`.
   - For example, your old template:
     ```html
     <div *ngFor="let item of count | numberRange">
       {{ item }}
     </div>
     ```
     Can be updated to the new syntax as follows:
     ```angular-ts
     @for (item of count | numberRange; track item) {
       .  <!-- content -->
     }
     ```

3. **For `ngElse`**:
   - Use `@else { ... }` after your `@if` block.
   - For example, if you have both conditions:
     ```angular-ts
     @if (isServerRunning) {
       Yes, the server is running
     }
     @else {
       No, the server is not running
     }
     ```

By following these steps, you can efficiently migrate your templates to the new syntax in Angular 17.

