1. **Wrap each iframe inside a `@defer` block** to defer its loading until necessary.
2. **Use Intersection Observer API** to detect when an iframe enters the viewport and trigger the `@defer` block.

Here's a simplified example:

```angular-html
<div *ngFor="let link of youtubeLinks; trackBy: trackById" #iframeRef>
  <div @defer [attr.id]="link.id">
    <iframe *ngIf="isIframeLoaded(link.id)" [src]="link.url"></iframe>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Intersection Observer setup -->
<ng-container *ngIf="observer">
  <ng-container *ngFor="let entry of observer.entries; trackBy: trackById">
    <ng-container *ngIf="entry.isIntersecting">
      @for (link of youtubeLinks; track $index) {
        <div [attr.id]="link.id" [@defer]></div>
      }
    </ng-container>
  </ng-container>
</ng-container>

<!-- Observer service or component setup -->
<ng-container *ngIf="!observer">
  <script>
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {
      entries.forEach(entry => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          // Trigger the @defer block for this entry
          // This part would be handled by Angular's change detection and the @defer directive
        }
      });
    });

    // Start observing elements as they are added to the DOM
  </script>
</ng-container>
```

In your component, you need to manage the `observer` setup and ensure that each iframe is loaded only when it enters the viewport. This approach ensures that iframes load progressively as needed, improving initial page load performance.

Remember to adjust the script part according to your specific requirements and Angular version compatibility.

