The error "localStorage is not defined" occurs because `localStorage` is a browser API and might not be available in non-browser environments like Node.js during testing or server-side rendering (SSR). To resolve this issue, you should provide a fallback mechanism for `localStorage`.

You can use the `providedIn: 'any'` strategy to ensure that your service works both client-side and server-side. Here's how you can modify your `AuthService`:

1. Update the `@Injectable` decorator to include `providedIn: 'any'`.
2. Provide a fallback implementation for `localStorage`.

Here’s an updated version of your `AuthService`:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Router } from '@angular/router';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'any', // Use 'any' to provide the service in both client and server contexts.
})
export class AuthService {
  public isAuth = new BehaviorSubject(false);

  constructor(private router: Router) {
    this.autoSignIn();
  }

  autoSignIn() {
    const email = localStorage.getItem('email'); // Fallback to a default value if not available.
    if (email) {
      this.isAuth.next(true);
      this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
    }
  }

  signIn(email: string) {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem('email', email); // Use try-catch for better error handling.
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("localStorage is not available", error);
    }
    this.isAuth.next(true);
    this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
  }

  signOut() {
    try {
      localStorage.removeItem('email'); // Use try-catch for better error handling.
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("localStorage is not available", error);
    }
    this.isAuth.next(false);
    this.router.navigate(['/auth']);
  }
}
```

By setting `providedIn: 'any'`, the service will work in both client and server contexts. The try-catch blocks help handle cases where `localStorage` might be unavailable, providing a more robust solution.

