In Angular 17, you can use `@if` and `@else` to conditionally render content without combining statements or using `||`. Here's how you can achieve the desired behavior:

```angular-ts
template: `
  @if (movies && movies.length > 0) {
    Favorites 
    Recent {{movies[movies.length-1].title}} - {{movies[movies.length-1].rating}}
    Total {{movies.length}}
  } @else {
    Favorites 
    Recent NA
    Total 0
  }
`
```

This template checks if `movies` exists and has items. If it does, it displays the recent movie details; otherwise, it shows "NA" for recent and "0" for total.

The key is to use separate `@if` and `@else` blocks to handle each condition distinctly, ensuring type safety without causing a `type never error`.

