To resolve the "Object is possibly 'undefined'" error in your template, you need to ensure that `posts` is not null or undefined before accessing its properties. You can use the safe navigation operator (`?.`) and type assertions where appropriate. Here’s how you can modify your template:

```html
@for(post of posts?.; track post.title){
  @if(post?.id%2==0){
    {{post?.id}}-{{post?.title}} 
  }
}
```

This change ensures that `posts` is checked for null or undefined before accessing its properties. If `posts` is null or undefined, the expressions inside the loop will not be evaluated.

Additionally, you can use non-null assertions (`!`) if you are certain that `post.id` and `post.title` will never be null in your component logic:

```html
@for(post of posts?.; track post.title){
  @if(post?.id%2==0){
    {{post!.id}}-{{post!.title}} 
  }
}
```

However, use non-null assertions sparingly as they can lead to runtime errors if the assertion is incorrect. It's generally better to handle potential null values gracefully in your template.

If you want to ensure that `posts` is always defined before rendering, consider initializing it with an empty array or object in your component:

```typescript
export class DashboardComponent {
  posts = []; // Initialize as an empty array
  // ... rest of the code ...
}
```

This approach avoids nullish coalescing issues and makes your template safer.

