The issue you're encountering arises from how Angular's template syntax handles `@if` within `@for`. The `@if` directive expects a single block of content, but your structure includes multiple blocks, leading to the error.

To achieve your goal, you can use the `@empty` directive in combination with `@for`, and conditionally render the table header and footer based on whether there is data. Here’s how you can do it:

```angular-ts
@Component({
  ...
  template: `
    @if (licenseOverview.orders.length > 0) {
      @for (order of licenseOverview.orders; track order.id; let firstRow = $first; let lastRow = $last) {
        @if(firstRow) {
          {{ 'Order' }} 
          {{ 'Expected On' }} 
          {{ 'Status' }} 
        }
        {{ order.reference }} 
        {{ ... }} 
      }
    } @empty { No data for you! }
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  licenseOverview = {
    orders: [
      // Your order data here
    ]
  };
}
```

This approach ensures that the table header is only rendered when there are orders, and `@empty` handles the case where no data exists.

