The issue arises from how you're handling the `selectAll` and `selectedChannels`, `selectedChatIds` states. To fix this, ensure each checkbox updates its own state independently. Here’s a revised approach:

1. **Update Form Data Structure**: Ensure your form data structure correctly handles individual selections.

2. **Checkbox Handling**: Use Vue's v-model to bind checkboxes directly to their respective states.

```vue
<template>
  <!-- Buttons Section -->
  <div v-for="button in buttons" :key="button.id">
    <input type="checkbox" 
           :value="button.buttonTitle"
           v-model="selectedButtons"
           @change="form.buttons = selectedButtons.map(b => b.buttonTitle)">
    {{ button.buttonTitle }}
  </div>
</template>

<script setup>
import { ref, watch } from 'vue';
import { useForm } from '@inertiajs/vue3';

const form = useForm({
  title: null,
  body: null,
  chatID: [],
  channelID: [],
  buttons: [], // Directly bind to this array
  images: [],
  date: null,
  time: null,
});

// Watch for changes in selectedButtons and update form.buttons accordingly
watch(selectedButtons, () => {
  form.buttons = selectedButtons.map(b => b.buttonTitle);
});
</script>
```

This ensures that each checkbox updates `selectedButtons` independently. When the state changes, it updates `form.buttons`, which is then sent to the server.

Ensure your controller and validation rules are correctly set up to handle arrays of button titles. This approach should resolve the issue where selecting one checkbox inadvertently selects all.

