To achieve your desired result in Laravel 10, you can use the `groupBy` and `sum` methods as shown below:

```php
$collection = collect([
    // Your array of objects here...
]);

$result = $collection->groupBy('barcode')
    ->map(function ($group, $key) {
        return [
            'id' => $group->first()['id'],
            'barcode' => $group->first()['barcode'],
            'description' => $group->first()['description'],
            'quantity' => $group->sum(fn($item) => $item['quantity']),
            'locations' => $group->unique('location')->pluck('location')->toArray(),
        ];
    })
    ->values();

// To separate by location, you can further process the result
$result = $result->map(function ($item) {
    foreach ($item['locations'] as $location) {
        yield [
            'id' => $item['id'],
            'barcode' => $item['barcode'],
            'description' => $item['description'],
            'quantity' => collect($group)->filter(fn($g) => $g['location'] == $location)->sum('quantity'),
            'location' => $location,
        ];
    }
});
```

This code first groups the collection by `barcode`, then sums up the quantities for each group. To separate them by location, you can further process the result as shown in the last part of the answer.

