To register middleware without making it global for specific routes, you don't need to modify `bootstrap/app.php`. Instead, you can directly apply middleware to your route definitions in your `routes/web.php` or `routes/api.php`.

For example, if you want to apply the `AdminCheck` middleware only to certain login-related routes, you would do something like this:

```php
use App\Http\Middleware\AdminCheck;

Route::prefix('login')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/', function () {
        // ...
    })->middleware('auth');

    Route::post('/login', [LoginController::class, 'login'])->middleware('admincheck');
});
```

In this example, the `AdminCheck` middleware is only applied to the `/login` POST route. The `auth` middleware is also applied to the login GET route for authentication purposes.

Regarding Laravel Breeze's `auth` middleware, it is a shorthand alias for the default authentication middleware provided by Laravel. You can find its definition in the `app/Http/Middleware/Authenticate.php` file or within the `vendor/laravel/framework/src/Illuminate/Foundation/Http/Middleware/Authenticate.php` directory if you want to explore it further.

This approach allows you to manage middleware on a per-route basis without affecting other routes globally.

